// Stories for the matrix: STORIES["<civ id>:<activity>"] = "…". See cultures/README.md.
Object.assign(STORIES, {
  // ---------------- Persians (Sasanian and later Iran) ----------------
  "persians:hunt": "In the great rock arch of Taq-e Bostan (طاق بستان), carved around 600 CE, Khosrow II (خسرو پرویز) stands in a reed boat while elephants drive wild boar through a marsh toward his bow. Other boats carry women playing harps, and the king's halo marks him as chosen. For the Sasanian court the hunt in the walled royal park, the pardis, was more than sport: a shah who mastered wild beasts showed that he could hold the world in order.",
  "persians:battle": "Near Edessa (ܐܘܪܗܝ) in 260 CE, Shapur I (شاپور) met the Roman army and took the emperor Valerian (Valerianus) himself prisoner. The savaran (سواران), horse and rider wrapped in mail, charged behind trumpets and drums, and the victory was cut into the cliff at Naqsh-e Rostam (نقش رستم): Shapur mounted, one Roman emperor kneeling before him, another held by the wrist. For Iran it was proof that the kingdom of Ahura Mazda (اهورامزدا) stood equal to Rome.",
  "persians:gather": "At Mehregan (مهرگان), the autumn feast of Mithra (مهر), the harvest of the Iranian plateau came in: wheat from fields watered by underground qanats, pomegranates, grapes, saffron picked thread by thread before dawn. Sasanian kings wore crowns shaped like the sun and received the gifts of the season, and people exchanged sweets and promises. Al-Biruni (البيروني) later wrote that after Nowruz (نوروز) it was the greatest feast of the Persians, a thanksgiving for a year that had not failed.",
  "persians:ritual": "Before a great war the Sasanian kings walked on foot, not riding, to Adur Gushnasp (آذرگشنسب), the fire temple of the warriors at Takht-e Soleyman (تخت سلیمان) beside its deep blue spring lake. Priests in white veils, mouths covered so their breath would not touch the flame, fed it sandalwood and recited the Avesta (اوستا). The king brought gifts and asked Ahura Mazda (اهورامزدا) for victory. No one shouted; the fire answered only by burning on.",
  "persians:rain": "In the Avesta (اوستا) the star Tishtrya (تیشتر), Sirius, fights the drought demon Apaosha (اپوش) at the edge of the sea: a white horse with golden ears against a black, bald horse. Three nights Tishtrya loses, because people forgot to pray; when they remember, he wins and the clouds roll over the land. Each summer at Tirgan (تیرگان), Iranian villages splashed water on one another and tied rainbow threads on their wrists, waiting for that victory.",
  "persians:flood": "Around 628 CE, in the last years of Khosrow II (خسرو پرویز), the Tigris (دجله) and Euphrates (فرات) rose higher than anyone remembered and broke the dykes of the Sawad (سواد), the rich black land of Mesopotamia. Al-Baladhuri (البلاذري) records that the king went himself to the breaches and had workers punished when the embankments failed again. They could not be closed; the water stayed for centuries as the Great Swamp, and the empire never recovered its old harvests.",
  "persians:wind": "In Sistan (سیستان), on Iran's eastern border, the wind of 120 days, bad-e sad-o-bist-ruz (باد صد و بیست روزه), blows every summer without stopping. It buries villages in sand and blinds travellers. People did not only suffer it: at Nashtifan (نشتیفان) the old vertical windmills of clay and wood, perhaps the oldest design in the world, still turn in it and grind grain. To live there was to make a partner of the storm.",
  "persians:fire": "At Sadeh (سده), fifty days before Nowruz (نوروز), Iranians lit huge bonfires to celebrate the discovery of fire. The Shahnameh (شاهنامه) tells how King Hushang (هوشنگ) threw a stone at a serpent, missed, struck a rock, and saw a spark leap out. He bowed to the light and ordered a feast. Fire was never worshipped as a god, but as the purest sign of Ahura Mazda (اهورامزدا), and the midwinter flames promised that the cold would end.",
  "persians:thunder": "Imagined: a Zagros (زاگرس) shepherd brings his flock down from the high pasture as black clouds pile over the peaks. Lightning cracks across the slopes, and he counts the seconds to the thunder, praying the storm will pass to the next valley. His grandmother says the sky is fighting the demons of drought, and that every peal is good news for the wheat below, even if it frightens the sheep.",
  "persians:birth": "The Shahnameh (شاهنامه) says Rudabeh (رودابه)'s labour with Rostam (رستم) was so hard she nearly died. Zal (زال) burned a feather of the Simurgh (سیمرغ), the great bird appeared, and told him to give her wine, open her side with a blade, lift the child out, and heal the wound with herbs and a stroke of the feather. Persians still call a caesarean birth rostamzad (رستم‌زاد). The baby, huge and crying, would one day save Iran again and again.",
  "persians:death": "A Zoroastrian funeral was quiet and careful. The body was washed, dressed in white, and shown to a dog, the sagdid (سگ‌دید), whose gaze was believed to drive away corruption. Bearers carried it in pairs to the dakhma (دخمه), the tower of silence, where sun and birds would return it to nature without defiling earth or fire. Mourners followed holding a cloth between them, and priests recited the Avesta (اوستا); on the fourth dawn the soul crossed the Chinvat Bridge (پل چینود).",

  // ---------------- Achaemenids ----------------
  "achaemenids:hunt": "On his cylinder seal, now in the British Museum, Darius the Great (𐎭𐎠𐎼𐎹𐎢𐎺𐎢𐏁) stands in a chariot under the winged disc of Ahura Mazda (𐎠𐎢𐎼𐎶𐏀𐎭𐎠), drawing his bow at a rearing lion. Persian kings hunted in vast walled parks the Greeks called paradeisoi (παράδεισοι); Xenophon (Ξενοφῶν) describes Cyrus the Younger riding down wild beasts at Celaenae (Κελαιναί) to train himself and his men. The hunt was the king's proof of courage, and the court's school of war.",
  "achaemenids:battle": "At Thermopylae (Θερμοπύλαι) in August 480 BCE, Xerxes (𐎧𐏁𐎹𐎠𐎼𐏁𐎠) waited four days for the small Greek army to withdraw. When the Medes and then his own Immortals were thrown back in the narrow pass, Herodotus (Ἡρόδοτος) says the king leapt three times from his throne in fear for his men. Then Ephialtes (Ἐφιάλτης) showed the mountain path, the Immortals marched through the night, and the pass fell. Victory came, but bitter and costly.",
  "achaemenids:gather": "On the staircase of the Apadana (𐎠𐎱𐎭𐎠𐎴) at Persepolis (𐎱𐎠𐎼𐎿), twenty-three peoples climb toward the king in stone: Lydians with gold bowls, Bactrians leading a two-humped camel, Ethiopians carrying an elephant tusk, Indians with spice jars on a yoke. Each delegation is led by the hand, peacefully. Darius (𐎭𐎠𐎼𐎹𐎢𐎺𐎢𐏁) wanted the gathering of the empire's harvests to look like a festival of many nations, not a list of the conquered.",
  "achaemenids:ritual": "Herodotus (Ἡρόδοτος) says that at sunrise before crossing the Hellespont (Ἑλλήσποντος) in 480 BCE, Xerxes (𐎧𐏁𐎹𐎠𐎼𐏁𐎠) poured wine from a golden cup into the sea, praying to the sun that nothing would stop him before he reached the ends of Europe. Then he threw the cup, a golden bowl and a Persian sword into the water. Myrtle branches were spread on the bridges, incense burned, and the army waited silently for the gods' answer.",
  "achaemenids:rain": "Imagined: in a dry year in Parsa (𐎱𐎠𐎼𐎿), villagers stand at their irrigation channels watching the mountains for cloud. In the city, a clerk notes grain rations on clay tablets that will survive for 2,500 years. Carved over the terrace of Persepolis is Darius's own prayer: may Ahura Mazda (𐎠𐎢𐎼𐎶𐏀𐎭𐎠) protect this land from the enemy army, from famine, and from the Lie. Everyone hopes the second wish is heard first.",
  "achaemenids:flood": "Herodotus (Ἡρόδοτος) tells that when Cyrus (𐎤𐎢𐎽𐎢𐏁) marched on Babylon (𒆍𒀭𒊏𒆠), one of his sacred white horses was swept away and drowned in the Gyndes (Γύνδης) river. Furious, Cyrus spent a whole summer having his army dig 360 channels until the river could be crossed by a woman without wetting her knees. Then, in 539 BCE, he diverted the Euphrates itself and his men walked into Babylon along the riverbed.",
  "achaemenids:wind": "In 524 BCE, Cambyses (𐎣𐎲𐎢𐎪𐎡𐎹) sent fifty thousand men from Thebes across the Western Desert to the oracle of Amun (𓇋𓏠𓈖) at Siwa. Herodotus (Ἡρόδοτος) says that half-way, as they ate their midday meal, a great south wind rose and buried them in sand. Not one returned. The Egyptians took it as the god's answer; for Persian families it was simply an army that vanished, and mothers who waited for sons who never came.",
  "achaemenids:fire": "In 330 BCE Alexander (Ἀλέξανδρος) set fire to the palaces of Persepolis (𐎱𐎠𐎼𐎿). Ancient writers say it happened at a drunken feast; others say it was revenge for the burning of Athens (Ἀθῆναι). Cedar roof beams brought from Lebanon fell blazing into the Hall of a Hundred Columns, and the archive of clay tablets was baked hard by the heat. For Persians it was the night the house of Darius (𐎭𐎠𐎼𐎹𐎢𐎺𐎢𐏁) ended.",
  "achaemenids:thunder": "In 480 BCE a Persian detachment marched on Delphi (Δελφοί) to take its treasures. Herodotus (Ἡρόδοτος), telling the Greek side, says that as they reached the temple of Athena Pronaia (Ἀθηνᾶ Προναία), thunderbolts fell from the sky, two crags broke from Parnassus (Παρνασσός) and crushed many, and a war cry was heard from inside the shrine. The survivors fled. Whatever truly happened, the soldiers knew mountain storms could be stronger than any king.",
  "achaemenids:birth": "Herodotus (Ἡρόδοτος) tells that King Astyages of the Medes dreamed his grandson would overthrow him and ordered the newborn killed. His steward Harpagus gave the baby to a herdsman, Mitradates, whose wife Spako had just borne a stillborn son. She begged to keep the living child and expose the dead one instead. The boy raised among the cattle was Cyrus (𐎤𐎢𐎽𐎢𐏁), who would found the greatest empire the world had seen.",
  "achaemenids:death": "Cyrus the Great (𐎤𐎢𐎽𐎢𐏁) was laid in a gold coffin in a small stone house on a stepped plinth at Pasargadae (Πασαργάδαι), around 530 BCE. Arrian (Ἀρριανός) says Magi guarded it, received a sheep daily and a horse each month to sacrifice for him. Plutarch (Πλούταρχος) records the inscription: O man, I am Cyrus, who won the Persians their empire; do not grudge me this little earth that covers my body. Alexander (Ἀλέξανδρος), finding it robbed, was moved.",

  // ---------------- Saracens (early Arab and medieval Arab peoples) ----------------
  "saracens:hunt": "Usama ibn Munqidh (أسامة بن منقذ), the 12th-century emir of Shayzar (شيزر) on the Orontes (العاصي), wrote fondly of his father's hunts: falcons and sakers on the wrist, cheetahs riding behind the saddle, salukis running down gazelle and hare on the plain. His father knew each hawk by name and wrote down every kill. In old age, Usama remembered those mornings as the happiest days of a life full of wars.",
  "saracens:battle": "In August 636, on the Yarmouk (اليرموك) river, the Arab armies under Khalid ibn al-Walid (خالد بن الوليد) faced the Byzantine host for six days in heat and dust. When the Arab lines bent back toward the camp, the women there, among them Hind bint Utbah (هند بنت عتبة), struck the retreating men with tent poles and drove them back to fight. On the last day the Byzantines were trapped in the ravine of Wadi al-Raqqad (وادي الرقاد), and Syria passed to Islam.",
  "saracens:gather": "In late summer the date harvest came to the oases of Medina (المدينة) and al-Ahsa (الأحساء). Climbers went up the palms with a rope belt, cut the heavy clusters and lowered them to people waiting below, while children gathered fallen fruit. The dates were dried and pressed into skins to feed caravans through the winter. In a land with little else, a good harvest meant guests could be fed and no one need go hungry.",
  "saracens:ritual": "Before a journey, a marriage or a campaign, a Muslim does not consult omens: he prays istikhara (استخارة). Two units of prayer, then the supplication the Prophet taught: O God, I ask You to choose for me by Your knowledge; if this matter is good for my faith, my life and my end, make it easy for me, and if it is bad, turn it away from me. Then he sleeps, and accepts what comes.",
  "saracens:rain": "In 638, the Year of Ashes (عام الرمادة), drought and hunger struck Arabia so hard that dust storms turned the earth grey. The caliph Umar ibn al-Khattab (عمر بن الخطاب) went out to pray the istisqa (استسقاء), the prayer for rain, holding the hand of al-Abbas (العباس), the Prophet's elderly uncle, and wept as he asked forgiveness. The reports say the clouds gathered before they left the place, and the people ran home through the first rain in months.",
  "saracens:wind": "In 627, during the Battle of the Trench (غزوة الخندق), ten thousand confederates besieged Medina (المدينة) for weeks. Then on a winter night a fierce, freezing wind tore through their camp, overturning cooking pots, blowing out fires and scattering tents. Abu Sufyan (أبو سفيان), their leader, mounted his camel and ordered the retreat. The Quran (القرآن) recalls it: We sent against them a wind and hosts you could not see. In Medina, the wind was mercy.",
  "saracens:fire": "On the hills of pre-Islamic Arabia, generous men lit nar al-qira (نار القرى), the fire of hospitality, so that lost travellers in the night would see it and come to eat. Hatim al-Ta'i (حاتم الطائي), remembered for centuries as the most generous of the Arabs, is said to have told his slave: light the fire high, and if it brings a guest, you are free. In the desert, a fire on a hill meant life.",
  "saracens:thunder": "The Quran (القرآن) says: the thunder glorifies His praise, and the angels also, in awe of Him. Desert Arabs watched far-off lightning, barq (برق), with longing, because it meant rain was falling somewhere. Imru' al-Qais (امرؤ القيس) ended his Mu'allaqa (المعلقة) watching such a storm at night, its lightning like a lamp in a monk's hand, the rain uprooting trees and pouring over the hills of Qatan (قطن) until the valleys ran.",
  "saracens:birth": "When a Muslim child is born, the father whispers the call to prayer into the right ear, so that the first words the baby hears are God is greatest. Someone pious chews a date and rubs it on the infant's palate, the tahnik (تحنيك). On the seventh day the hair is shaved, its weight in silver is given to the poor, and a sheep is sacrificed for the aqiqah (عقيقة). No one knows what this child will become; everyone prays it will be good.",
  "saracens:death": "An Islamic funeral has no music. The body is washed an odd number of times, wrapped in plain white cloth, and carried quickly, the same day if possible, by men who take turns at the bier. The prayer for the dead is said standing, without bowing. In the grave the body lies on its right side, facing Mecca (مكة). Loud wailing is discouraged; quiet tears are not. Khalid ibn al-Walid (خالد بن الوليد), scarred by a hundred battles, died in bed at Homs (حمص) and wept that he had not died a martyr.",

  // ---------------- Abbasid Dynasty ----------------
  "abbasids:hunt": "The Abbasid caliphs at Samarra (سامراء) kept great walled game reserves, the hayr (الحير), stocked with gazelle, onager and lions. They rode out with cheetahs on cushions behind the saddle, falcons on gloved fists and long-legged salukis. Abu Nuwas (أبو نواس), the wittiest poet of Baghdad (بغداد), invented a whole genre for it, the tardiyyat (طرديات), hunting poems that praise a hound's speed and a falcon's eye as lovingly as wine.",
  "abbasids:battle": "In 837 Byzantine raiders sacked the frontier town of Zapetra (زبطرة), and a captive Muslim woman was said to have cried out: O Mu'tasim! When the caliph heard, he swore to answer. Astrologers warned the stars were against a campaign, but in August 838 al-Mu'tasim (المعتصم) took Amorium (عمورية), the Byzantine emperor's ancestral city. Abu Tammam (أبو تمام) celebrated it in a famous ode: the sword is truer than the books.",
  "abbasids:gather": "Around Baghdad (بغداد) lay the Sawad (السواد), the black land between the rivers, crossed by canals. Under the Abbasids farmers grew wheat and barley, dates, rice, sugar cane, cotton and lemons brought from India and Persia. At harvest the tax-collectors measured the kharaj (خراج), and barges carried grain down the canals to the Round City (المدينة المدورة). Ibn Wahshiyya (ابن وحشية)'s Nabataean Agriculture (الفلاحة النبطية) recorded their know-how, proud that the old soil still fed an empire.",
  "abbasids:ritual": "When al-Mansur (المنصور) chose the site of Baghdad (بغداد) by the Tigris (دجلة), he did not begin the work lightly. The court astrologers Nawbakht (نوبخت) and Masha'allah (ما شاء الله) calculated the moment, and on 30 July 762 the first brick was laid with a prayer. The caliph traced the walls of the Round City in lines of ash and set them alight at night so he could see its plan. He named it Madinat al-Salam (مدينة السلام), the City of Peace.",
  "abbasids:rain": "A famous story says Harun al-Rashid (هارون الرشيد) once looked up at a passing cloud and said to it: rain wherever you wish, your harvest will come back to me. It was a boast about the reach of the caliphate, from North Africa to Central Asia. But farmers along the Tigris (دجلة) knew no caliph commanded the sky, and in dry winters they gathered in the mosques for the prayer for rain like everyone else.",
  "abbasids:flood": "Baghdad (بغداد) lived between two rivers and paid for it. Chroniclers such as Ibn al-Jawzi (ابن الجوزي) recorded year after year when the Tigris (دجلة) rose in spring, broke its banks and poured into the city, collapsing mud-brick houses, flooding cemeteries and turning markets into lakes. People carried their families onto rooftops and prayed. Each time, when the water fell, they rebuilt on the same ground, because it was still Baghdad.",
  "abbasids:wind": "Imagined: on a summer afternoon a dust storm rolls in from the western desert. The sky over Baghdad (بغداد) turns yellow, then red, then dark as night. Merchants pull shutters across their shops on the Karkh (الكرخ) market, boatmen tie up on the Tigris (دجلة), and scholars in the House of Wisdom (بيت الحكمة) cover their manuscripts with cloth. When it passes, everything is coated in fine dust, and the muezzin calls as if nothing happened.",
  "abbasids:fire": "In February 1258 the Mongol army of Hulagu (Хүлэгү) broke into Baghdad (بغداد). For days the city burned. The caliph al-Musta'sim (المستعصم) was killed, and the great libraries, including the House of Wisdom (بيت الحكمة), were destroyed; chroniclers said so many books were thrown into the Tigris (دجلة) that its water ran black with ink, then red with blood. For Muslims everywhere it was the end of a world that had lasted five hundred years.",
  "abbasids:thunder": "Imagined: a winter storm breaks over Baghdad (بغداد). Thunder rolls across the domes and the children of a scholar's house run to him in fear. He tells them what al-Kindi (الكندي) wrote, that thunder and lightning come from vapours colliding in the clouds, and then adds the verse every child knows: the thunder glorifies His praise. Science and prayer sit side by side, and the children fall asleep to the rain.",
  "abbasids:birth": "In September 786, on a single night, the caliph al-Hadi (الهادي) died, his brother Harun al-Rashid (هارون الرشيد) became caliph, and Harun's son Abdallah, later al-Ma'mun (المأمون), was born. Baghdad (بغداد) called it the night of three caliphs. A child born that night seemed blessed beyond doubt. Yet years later he would fight a civil war against his half-brother al-Amin (الأمين), and also found the greatest age of science in Islam.",
  "abbasids:death": "In 809 Harun al-Rashid (هارون الرشيد) fell ill on campaign near Tus (طوس) in Khorasan (خراسان). Knowing he would die, he had his grave dug in a garden there and looked down into it, saying: son of Adam, this is your end. He was washed, shrouded and prayed over by his son Salih, and buried quickly, as Muslims are, without music. The caliph of the Thousand and One Nights (ألف ليلة وليلة) lies in a simple grave far from Baghdad (بغداد).",

  // ---------------- Ayyubids ----------------
  "ayyubids:hunt": "Imagined: in the green Ghouta (الغوطة) outside Damascus (دمشق), a young Ayyubid prince rides out at dawn with his falconers. A saker leaves the glove, climbs, and stoops on a crane over the Barada (بردى) river. The old huntsman praises the bird, not the prince. They return at noon with cranes and hares for the evening meal, and the prince's uncle reminds him that hunting is only practice for the war with the Franks.",
  "ayyubids:battle": "On 4 July 1187, at the Horns of Hattin (قرون حطين) above the Sea of Galilee (بحيرة طبريا), Saladin (صلاح الدين) trapped the crusader army without water. His men set the dry grass alight so smoke blew into the thirsty knights' faces. The True Cross was captured and King Guy surrendered. In his tent Saladin handed Guy a cup of iced rose-water, and when Guy passed it on to Reynald of Chatillon (Renaud de Châtillon), Saladin said: I did not give it to him.",
  "ayyubids:gather": "In the Ghouta (الغوطة), the oasis of orchards around Damascus (دمشق), the apricot harvest came in early summer. Families climbed into the trees, and the fruit was dried in sheets on rooftops into qamar al-din (قمر الدين), apricot leather, to be sold across the Ayyubid lands. In Egypt the sugar cane was cut and pressed in sultans' mills. Under Saladin (صلاح الدين)'s heirs, peace with the Franks meant harvests and trade could flourish.",
  "ayyubids:ritual": "Baha al-Din ibn Shaddad (بهاء الدين بن شداد), Saladin (صلاح الدين)'s judge and biographer, writes that the sultan liked to begin battles on a Friday, at the hour of the Friday prayer, so that preachers in every mosque would be praying for the Muslim armies at the very moment they met the enemy. He prayed himself before every engagement, and never consulted astrologers. The answer, he believed, belonged to God alone.",
  "ayyubids:rain": "Imagined: a dry winter in Damascus (دمشق), the Barada (بردى) river low between its banks. The people go out to the musalla (مصلى) outside the city, dressed in old clothes, humble, the old and the children with them. The imam leads the prayer for rain and turns his cloak inside out, as the Prophet did, hoping the sky will turn too. On the walk home, a wind brings the smell of wet dust.",
  "ayyubids:flood": "In 1221 the army of the Fifth Crusade marched up the Nile (النيل) from Damietta (دمياط) toward Cairo (القاهرة), just as the annual flood was rising. Sultan al-Kamil (الكامل) let them pass, then had the dykes behind them cut. The water surrounded the crusader camp and turned their road into a swamp. Starving and trapped, they surrendered Damietta in exchange for safe passage. The Nile itself had fought for Egypt.",
  "ayyubids:wind": "Imagined: in spring the khamsin (خماسين) blows out of the Western Desert into Cairo (القاهرة), a hot wind loaded with sand. The sky turns orange over the Citadel that Saladin (صلاح الدين) built on the Muqattam (المقطم) hills. Mamluk guards wrap their faces, camels kneel with their backs to the wind, and in the madrasas the students cannot read for the darkness. The old say it will blow for fifty days, but tomorrow may be clear.",
  "ayyubids:fire": "During the siege of Acre (عكا) in 1190, the crusaders built three great siege towers that no fire could burn. Ibn Shaddad (ابن شداد) tells how a young coppersmith from Damascus (دمشق) offered a mixture of naphtha and other substances he had prepared. He threw a pot at one tower: nothing. Then another, and it burst into flame, then the next. The Muslims on the walls shouted with joy; Saladin (صلاح الدين) wanted to reward him, but he refused.",
  "ayyubids:thunder": "Imagined: a thunderstorm breaks over the Ayyubid camp outside Acre (عكا) in winter. Tents collapse, horses scream, the trenches fill with water. Saladin (صلاح الدين), ill with fever, walks among the soldiers to see they are sheltered. Across the field the crusader camp suffers worse. Both armies hear the same thunder, and in both, men pray that the siege will end before they do.",
  "ayyubids:birth": "Saladin (صلاح الدين) was born in Tikrit (تكريت) in 1137 or 1138, on the very night his father Ayyub (أيوب) and uncle Shirkuh (شيركوه) were forced to flee the town after Shirkuh killed a man. Ayyub thought the child had brought bad luck and wanted nothing to do with him. One of his men answered: you do not know, this child may become a great and famous king. Fifty years later he took Jerusalem (القدس).",
  "ayyubids:death": "Saladin (صلاح الدين) died in Damascus (دمشق) on 4 March 1193, after twelve days of fever. Ibn Shaddad (ابن شداد) wrote that the treasury held only forty-seven dirhams and one gold piece; he had given everything away, and money had to be borrowed for his funeral. He was washed and shrouded, prayed over, and buried that afternoon. There was no music, only the weeping of a city that felt it had lost a father.",

  // ---------------- Turks (Seljuks and older Turkic peoples) ----------------
  "turks:hunt": "Malik-Shah (ملکشاه), the Seljuk sultan who ruled from Anatolia to Central Asia in the 1070s and 1080s, loved hunting above everything. Chroniclers say he counted his kills and gave a gold dinar to the poor for every animal he took, so that his pleasure would be paid for in charity. At Kufa (الكوفة) he raised a tower studded with the horns and hooves of the gazelles he had shot.",
  "turks:battle": "On Friday 26 August 1071, at Manzikert (Malazgirt), Sultan Alp Arslan (آلپ ارسلان) prayed with his army, dressed in white, and said it would be his shroud if he fell. He tied up his horse's tail with his own hand. The Seljuk horse archers drew the Byzantine army forward, then closed around it. Emperor Romanos IV (Ῥωμανὸς Διογένης) was captured, and Alp Arslan treated him with honour and let him go. Anatolia opened to the Turks.",
  "turks:gather": "Each spring the Turkmen clans of Anatolia moved their black goat-hair tents up to the yayla, the high summer pastures. Women milked ewes and made yogurt, cheese and butter, and wove the knotted carpets that would be sold in Konya. In autumn they came down again with wool, felt and dried curds. The yearly migration was the harvest, and the old Oghuz (Oğuz) songs went up and down with it.",
  "turks:ritual": "The Byzantine historian Menander (Μένανδρος), writing of the Turks of the 560s, says they honoured fire, water and earth, but worshipped only the creator of heaven and earth, Tengri (𐱅𐰭𐰼𐰃), sacrificing horses, cattle and sheep to him. Their shamans, kam, foretold the future. On the Orkhon stones the khagans say they ruled because Tengri willed it, and that when the people forgot him, they were lost.",
  "turks:rain": "The old Turkic peoples knew the yada tash (yada taş), the rain stone. A yadachi, a rain-maker, would dip the stone in water and chant, calling clouds, snow or storm. Mahmud al-Kashgari (محمود الكاشغري) in 1072 wrote that he had seen it himself: a man brought snow and rain in summer with such a stone and put out a fire. For herders on the dry steppe, it was the most precious magic of all.",
  "turks:flood": "Imagined: in spring the snow melts on the Taurus (Toros) mountains and the river below the Turkmen village runs wild, brown and roaring. The flood carries off a mill and a flock of sheep, and the men stand on the bank with ropes, unable to do anything. The old woman says the river spirit is hungry this year. In the evening they cook a lamb and share it with the whole village, for luck.",
  "turks:wind": "Imagined: a dust storm crosses the plain of Konya in late summer. The caravan stops at the Sultan Han, the great stone caravanserai built by the Seljuk sultan Kayqubad (کیقباد) in 1229. Camels are led through the carved gate, merchants crowd into the vaulted hall, and the iron doors are shut. Outside the wind screams for hours. Inside, someone tells stories of Dede Korkut until it passes.",
  "turks:fire": "In 569 the Byzantine envoy Zemarchus (Ζήμαρχος) reached the court of the Turkic khagan Istemi in the Altai. Menander (Μένανδρος) records that before he could enter, Turkic shamans beat drums and rang bells over his baggage, carried burning frankincense around him, and made him walk between two fires to purify him of evil. Fire, for the old Turks, cleansed whatever came from outside.",
  "turks:thunder": "Imagined: on the open steppe, a herder sees the storm before he hears it. The old Turks believed thunder was Tengri (𐱅𐰭𐰼𐰃)'s voice and that lightning struck where something impure lay. When a tree or a tent was struck, the place was left alone and offerings were made. He brings his horses together, ties them, and waits on foot, small under the enormous sky, until the rain arrives.",
  "turks:birth": "Old Turkic mothers called on Umay (𐰆𐰢𐰖), the goddess of birth and children. The Orkhon inscription of Kul Tigin (𐰚𐰇𐰠𐱅𐰃𐰏𐰤) says his mother was like Umay. In the yurt a woman in labour held a rope hung from the roof frame, while midwives and the women of the family helped, and the afterbirth was buried with care, since Umay was believed to guard it. Then the child was given a name, and the clan wondered what it would become.",
  "turks:death": "Chinese chronicles describe the Göktürk funeral. The body was laid in a tent, and relatives rode seven times around it, crying out and slashing their faces with knives so that blood ran with tears. On a chosen day the dead was burned with his horse and goods, and a stone statue raised over the grave, with a line of stones, balbals, one for each enemy he had killed. Kul Tigin (𐰚𐰇𐰠𐱅𐰃𐰏𐰤) was mourned this way in 731.",

  // ---------------- Ottomans ----------------
  "ottomans:hunt": "Sultan Mehmed IV (محمد رابع), who reigned from 1648 to 1687, was so devoted to the chase that he was called Avci (آوجی), the Hunter. From his palace at Edirne (ادرنه) he rode out for weeks at a time, and villagers by the thousands were ordered to beat the forests, driving deer, boar and wolves toward him. Courtiers grumbled that he hunted while the empire's wars went badly; the sultan answered that the hunt kept him strong for the saddle.",
  "ottomans:battle": "Before dawn on 29 May 1453, Mehmed II (محمد ثانی) launched the final assault on Constantinople (قسطنطنیه). The mehter (مهتر) band played on behind the lines, kettledrums and zurnas sounding without pause so that the soldiers would not lose heart. Weeks earlier his ships had been dragged overland on greased logs into the Golden Horn (Haliç). When the Janissaries (Yeniçeri) broke through near the Gate of St Romanus, the city of a thousand years fell. The sultan was twenty-one.",
  "ottomans:gather": "Every June the silkworm cocoons of the Bursa (بورسه) countryside came in. Farmers carried them in sacks to the Koza Han, the Cocoon Inn built in 1491 by Bayezid II (بایزید ثانی), where merchants from Persia, Italy and the whole empire bargained in the courtyard around the small mosque. The cocoons were boiled, the threads reeled, and the silk woven into the velvets and brocades that dressed the Ottoman court.",
  "ottomans:ritual": "When the sultan went to war, the Sancak-i Serif (سنجاق شریف), the holy banner said to have belonged to the Prophet, was brought out of its chest in Topkapi Palace (Topkapı Sarayı). The Quran (قرآن) was recited, the ulema prayed, and the sultan or grand vizier received the banner with tears and kissed it. It was carried at the head of the army. Muslims did not ask fortune-tellers about the campaign; they asked God, and accepted His answer.",
  "ottomans:rain": "In years of drought, the people of Istanbul (استانبول) climbed to the open-air prayer ground at Okmeydani (Okmeydanı), above the Golden Horn (Haliç). Scholars, artisans, children and even animals were brought along, because Muslims believed God might show mercy for the sake of the innocent. They prayed the yagmur duasi (yağmur duası), the prayer for rain, and the preachers urged everyone to repay debts and make peace. Evliya Celebi (Evliya Çelebi) describes these crowds walking home hopeful.",
  "ottomans:flood": "In September 1563, a sudden flood swept down the valleys north of Istanbul (استانبول), tearing through the Kagithane (Kâğıthane) stream and destroying parts of the aqueducts that carried water to the city. Houses and mills were washed away, and many drowned. Suleiman the Magnificent (سلیمان قانونی) ordered his architect Mimar Sinan (معمار سنان) to rebuild the Kirkcesme (Kırkçeşme) water system, and Sinan raised great stone aqueducts across the valleys, some still standing today.",
  "ottomans:wind": "In October 1541 the emperor Charles V landed a huge army outside Algiers (الجزائر), defended by Hasan Agha (حسن آغا) for the Ottoman sultan. Then a terrible storm blew in from the sea. Rain soaked the Spanish gunpowder, and over a hundred and fifty ships were driven onto the shore and wrecked. The defenders sallied out, and the emperor had to sail away. In Algiers the storm was remembered as God's help to the faithful.",
  "ottomans:fire": "Istanbul (استانبول) was a city of wooden houses, and its great fear was the cry yangin var (yangın var), there is fire. In July 1660 a fire began near Eminonu (Eminönü) and burned for days, destroying much of the city, mosques, markets and tens of thousands of homes. The mother of the sultan, Turhan Hatice, used the ruins to complete the New Mosque (Yeni Cami) at the waterfront. Later the tulumbaci (tulumbacı) firefighters ran through the streets carrying pumps on their shoulders.",
  "ottomans:thunder": "Imagined: a summer storm crashes over the Bosphorus (Boğaziçi). Lightning strikes the sea near Uskudar (Üsküdar), and fishermen row hard for shore. In Topkapi (Topkapı), the pages of the palace school stop their calligraphy lesson and look out over the water. Their old teacher says nothing; he only finishes the letter he was drawing, then recites a prayer for those at sea. When the thunder fades, the lesson continues.",
  "ottomans:birth": "When a son was born to the sultan, cannons fired from Topkapi (Topkapı), and Istanbul (استانبول) was illuminated with lamps for days; the crowd cheered. But everyone knew what being a prince meant. Under the law of fratricide, when Mehmed III (محمد ثالث) became sultan in 1595, his nineteen brothers were strangled. A prince's birth was the hope of the dynasty and also its fear, and his mother prayed both that he would rule and that he would live.",
  "ottomans:death": "Suleiman the Magnificent (سلیمان قانونی) died in his tent during the siege of Szigetvar (Szigetvár) in September 1566. The grand vizier Sokollu Mehmed Pasha (Sokollu Mehmed Paşa) hid his death for weeks so the army would not lose heart, until the new sultan was secure. The body was carried home, washed and shrouded, and prayed over at the Suleymaniye Mosque (Süleymaniye Camii), then laid in its tomb. As with every Muslim funeral there was no music, only recitation and prayer.",

  // ---------------- Armenians ----------------
  "armenians:hunt": "Movses Khorenatsi (Մովսես Խորենացի) tells of King Artavazd (Արտավազդ), cursed by his dying father. One day while hunting on the slopes of Mount Masis (Մասիս), Ararat (Արարատ), he was seized by madness, his horse plunged into a chasm, and he vanished. Armenian villagers said he was chained in a cave, guarded by dogs who licked his chains thin, and that blacksmiths struck their anvils each Sunday to keep him bound. The hunt became a warning.",
  "armenians:battle": "On 26 May 451, at Avarayr (Ավարայր), Vardan Mamikonian (Վարդան Մամիկոնյան) led the Armenians against the Sasanian Persians, who demanded they give up Christianity. The Armenians were defeated and Vardan fell with many companions. Yet the resistance went on until Persia granted freedom of religion in the treaty of Nvarsak (Նվարսակ) in 484. The church honours them each year at Vardanants (Վարդանանք): a lost battle remembered as a victory of conscience.",
  "armenians:gather": "Armenians say Noah (Նոյ) planted the first vineyard below Ararat (Արարատ) when the ark came to rest. In the Areni-1 (Արենի) cave archaeologists found a winery more than 6,000 years old, with a pressing basin and storage jars. Each autumn the vintage still comes in: families cut grapes on the terraces, press them, and bless the first wine. At the feast of the Assumption the grapes are blessed in church before anyone eats them.",
  "armenians:ritual": "The Armenian Church keeps the matagh (մատաղ), an offering of a lamb or rooster on behalf of the sick, the dead, or someone setting out on a journey. A priest blesses salt, the animal is given salt to eat, and then it is slaughtered and the meat shared among the poor and neighbours. Nothing is burned on an altar; the offering is the meal shared, and the prayer that God will look kindly on what comes.",
  "armenians:rain": "In high summer, when the fields of the Ararat (Արարատ) plain are parched, Armenians celebrate Vardavar (Վարդավառ). Once it honoured the goddess Astghik (Աստղիկ); now it is the feast of the Transfiguration. People throw buckets of water over one another in the streets, children ambush adults, and no one can be angry. It is laughter, but also an old wish that the sky will do the same, and that rain will come before the wheat is lost.",
  "armenians:wind": "In the winter of 401 BCE, Xenophon (Ξενοφῶν)'s Greek army crossed the Armenian highlands in a north wind so fierce that men lost toes to frostbite and snow lay deep. They sacrificed to the wind. Then they found Armenian villages with houses dug underground, where families lived beside their goats and cows and served barley beer drunk through straws. The villagers had learned long before how to wait out such storms.",
  "armenians:fire": "On Trndez (Տրնդեզ), in February, Armenians light a great bonfire in the churchyard from candles blessed in church. Newlyweds jump over it hand in hand, then young people who hope to marry, then everyone else; women who want children walk around it. The ashes are scattered on the fields for fertility. Fire, here, means warmth returning after winter, and the promise of the coming spring.",
  "armenians:thunder": "In the old Armenian faith, Vahagn (Վահագն) was the god of thunder and war, the dragon-slayer, Vishapakagh (Վիշապաքաղ). When lightning split the summer sky over the mountains, it was Vahagn striking the vishaps (վիշապներ), the dragons who swallowed rain and hid it in the clouds. Stone dragon-fish, vishapakar (վիշապաքար), still stand by highland springs. The thunder was frightening, but it meant the waters were being freed.",
  "armenians:birth": "Movses Khorenatsi (Մովսես Խորենացի) preserved an ancient song once sung with a lyre: heaven and earth were in labour, the purple sea was in labour, and a little red reed in the sea. From the reed's stem came smoke, from it flame, and from the flame a little boy ran, with hair of fire and a beard of flame, and his eyes were suns. It was the birth of Vahagn (Վահագն), and of every Armenian hope.",
  "armenians:death": "After an Armenian death, the family keeps the body at home for a night with candles; women sing laments for the dead by name. The priest leads the requiem, the hogehangist (հոգեհանգիստ), and the dead are buried facing east. Over the centuries Armenians carved khachkars (խաչքարեր), stone crosses laced like embroidery, for the salvation of a soul. At Noratus (Նորատուս) by Lake Sevan (Սևանա լիճ), nearly a thousand stand together in the grass.",

  // ---------------- Georgians ----------------
  "georgians:hunt": "Shota Rustaveli (შოთა რუსთაველი)'s Knight in the Panther's Skin (ვეფხისტყაოსანი), written around 1200 for Queen Tamar (თამარი), opens with a hunt. King Rostevan (როსტევანი) of Arabia and his young commander Avtandil (ავთანდილი) compete with bows across the plain, servants counting every kill, and the king laughs when the young man wins. Then they see a stranger weeping by a stream in a panther's skin, and the adventure begins. Georgians still learn its verses by heart.",
  "georgians:battle": "On 12 August 1121, at Didgori (დიდგორი), King David IV the Builder (დავით აღმაშენებელი) met a far larger Seljuk coalition. He had reformed his army, and two hundred of his men rode forward as if to surrender, then struck the enemy commanders. Georgian cavalry charged from the hills, and the coalition broke. Georgians call it the miraculous victory, dzlevai sakvirveli (ძლევაჲ საკვირველი). A year later David took Tbilisi (თბილისი) back, and the golden age began.",
  "georgians:gather": "Every autumn is rtveli (რთველი), the grape harvest. Whole villages in Kakheti (კახეთი) go into the vineyards together; the grapes are trodden in a wooden trough, and the juice, skins and stems poured into a qvevri (ქვევრი), a huge clay jar buried in the earth, sealed to ferment until spring. Afterwards comes the supra (სუფრა), a feast led by a tamada (თამადა), toastmaster, and three-part songs that rise until late at night.",
  "georgians:ritual": "In the mountains of Khevsureti (ხევსურეთი), each community had its khati (ხატი), a shrine, served by a khevisberi (ხევისბერი), a priest chosen from the village elders. At the festival he brewed sacred beer, slaughtered sheep, and spoke to the shrine's divine patron for everyone. Sometimes a kadagi (კადაგი), a seer, fell into a trance and spoke the deity's will about the coming year. People listened in silence, men on one side, women at a distance.",
  "georgians:rain": "In Georgian villages, during a drought, children made a doll called Lazare (ლაზარე) or Gonja (გონჯა) from a stick dressed in rags and carried it from house to house singing, Lazare, give us rain. Each family poured water over the doll and gave the children eggs, flour or cheese. Then the doll was thrown into a river. It was play, and also a real prayer by people whose bread depended on the sky.",
  "georgians:flood": "Imagined: in spring the Rioni (რიონი) river, the ancient Phasis (Φᾶσις) of Colchis (Κολχίς), swells with snowmelt from the Caucasus. The water spreads over the lowland fields and the farmers of Imereti (იმერეთი) watch from their wooden houses raised on posts. They pray to St George (წმინდა გიორგი) and wait. When the river withdraws it leaves fresh silt, and the maize will grow tall. The flood takes, and it gives.",
  "georgians:wind": "Imagined: a winter storm comes over the passes into Upper Svaneti (ზემო სვანეთი). Snow drives sideways and wind howls through the gorges. Each Svan family climbs into its stone tower, built a thousand years ago for defence against enemies, avalanches and blood feuds. Up there, by a small fire, the grandfather sings a slow song with his sons in three voices, and the wind outside becomes part of the harmony.",
  "georgians:fire": "In September 1795 the Persian shah Agha Mohammad Khan (آغا محمدخان) defeated the small Georgian army at Krtsanisi (კრწანისი) and entered Tbilisi (თბილისი). The city was burned and plundered for days; churches were destroyed and thousands of people taken captive. King Erekle II (ერეკლე II), seventy-five years old, had to be dragged from the battlefield by his grandson. Georgians remember the three hundred Aragvians (არაგველები) who fought to the last on the field.",
  "georgians:thunder": "In the mountains of Svaneti (სვანეთი), lightning belonged to the prophet Elijah (ელია). When someone was struck and killed, the Svans did not mourn in the usual way, because the dead had been chosen by God. They danced slowly in a circle around the body, singing Elia lrde, an ancient hymn to Elijah, asking him to spare the living. Thunder in the high valleys was holy, terrible and close.",
  "georgians:birth": "In the high Caucasus, Khevsur (ხევსური) and Pshav women gave birth in a small separate hut, the samrevlo, outside the village, because birth was considered impure for the shrine. They often laboured alone or with one helper. It was dangerous, and many died. Yet when a child came home, the family toasted it at the supra (სუფრა): may you be a good Georgian, may you be the one who brings our people luck.",
  "georgians:death": "When someone dies in Svaneti (სვანეთი), the village gathers for the zari (ზარი), the funeral chant: men sing in three voices a slow, harsh cry of ah and vai, almost without words, a sound some think older than Christianity. Women sit by the body and lament its name. After the burial comes the kelekhi (ქელეხი), a memorial feast, with toasts to the dead and to the ancestors who receive them.",

  // ---------------- Huns ----------------
  "huns:hunt": "Jordanes (Iordanes) tells an old story of how the Huns came west. Hunters on the far side of the Maeotic marshes, the Sea of Azov, followed a hind that kept stopping and looking back. She led them through shallows no one had known could be crossed, and then vanished. On the other side lay Scythia (Σκυθία), rich and open. The hunters rode home, called their people, and a new age of the steppe began.",
  "huns:battle": "In 452 Attila besieged Aquileia in northern Italy for a long time, and his men began to grumble and want to leave. Then, Priscus (Πρίσκος) and Jordanes say, Attila saw storks that nested on the city's roofs carrying their young out into the countryside. He told his army: look, the birds know this city will fall. The Huns took heart, brought up their siege engines, and Aquileia was taken and destroyed.",
  "huns:gather": "In 449 the Roman envoy Priscus (Πρίσκος) rode through Hunnic lands to Attila's court. In the villages, he wrote, people gave them millet instead of wheat, and a drink they called medos (μέδος), mead, while the servants drank kamon (κάμος), made from barley. At Attila's wooden hall, guests were served on silver, but Attila himself ate meat from a wooden plate and wore plain clothes. Gathering and sharing was how power was shown.",
  "huns:ritual": "Before the battle of the Catalaunian Plains (Campi Catalaunici) in 451, Jordanes (Iordanes) writes, Attila ordered his soothsayers to read the future in the entrails of cattle and in the streaks on scraped bones. They foretold disaster for the Huns, but said a great enemy leader would fall. Attila decided the price was worth paying. He fought, and Theodoric, king of the Visigoths, was killed. The prophecy had told the truth both ways.",
  "huns:rain": "Imagined: a hot, dry summer on the Pannonian plain. The grass is brown, the horses thin, and the herds must move to find water. Hunnic families pack their wagons and drive the animals toward the Tisza river. At night they watch the western sky for lightning. An old woman ties a strip of cloth to a tree and whispers to the sky that the children need the rain.",
  "huns:wind": "Imagined: a steppe storm rolls in from the east. A wall of dust darkens the sun, and the Hunnic camp moves fast: wagons are drawn into a circle, felt covers lashed down, horses turned with their tails to the wind. The riders cover their faces and wait. They are not afraid; the wind has always come with them, and the Romans say the Huns themselves came like a storm.",
  "huns:fire": "After the battle of the Catalaunian Plains (Campi Catalaunici) in 451, Attila withdrew into his camp behind a ring of wagons. Jordanes (Iordanes) says he ordered a pyre built of horse saddles, so that if the Romans broke in, he would throw himself into the flames rather than be captured or killed by an enemy. The fire was never lit; the Romans and Goths let him go. But the saddles stood ready all night.",
  "huns:thunder": "Imagined: on the great plain a thunderstorm breaks over the Hunnic camp. The horses stamp and scream, and lightning splits a lone tree near the river. The shamans say the sky god is angry and must be calmed with a horse sacrifice. Children hide under the wagons, counting the flashes. By morning the grass is green again, and the storm is told around the fire as a story of power.",
  "huns:birth": "Priscus (Πρίσκος), at Attila's banquet in 449, noticed that the king, stern and unsmiling with everyone else, drew his youngest son Ernak (Ἠρνάχ) close and looked at him with gentle eyes, pinching his cheek. A Hun explained: the soothsayers had told Attila that his family would fall, but would be restored by this boy. Every father hopes his child will save the house; Attila had been told so, and believed it.",
  "huns:death": "Attila died in 453 on the night of his wedding. Jordanes (Iordanes), following Priscus (Πρίσκος), says his body lay in a silk tent in the middle of the plain while the best horsemen rode around it singing a funeral song, the strava, of his deeds. His men cut off their hair and gashed their faces, so that the greatest warrior was mourned not with tears but with blood. He was buried secretly in three coffins of gold, silver and iron.",

  // ---------------- Cumans (Kipchaks) ----------------
  "cumans:hunt": "Imagined: a Cuman khan rides out on the Pontic steppe with his falconers and a golden eagle on a leather glove. Dogs flush a fox from the feather-grass, the eagle drops on it from a great height, and the men cheer. Hunting kept the young riders sharp for war and filled the pots in winter. Behind them, on a kurgan, a stone statue of an ancestor watches the plain.",
  "cumans:battle": "In 1185 Prince Igor (Игорь) of Novgorod-Seversk (Новгород-Северский) rode against the Cumans, the Polovtsians (половци), to win glory. The Cuman khans Konchak and Gzak gathered their warriors and surrounded his army at the river Kayala (Каяла). After three days of fighting without water, the Rus were destroyed and Igor taken captive. Konchak treated him as a guest and later married his daughter to Igor's son. The Lay of Igor's Campaign (Слово о плъку Игоревѣ) mourns the defeat; the Cumans celebrated.",
  "cumans:gather": "Imagined: in early summer, the Cuman women milk the mares in the camp. The foals are tied in a line so the mares stay near, and the milk is poured into a leather bag and churned with a wooden plunger for days until it becomes kumis, sour and lightly alcoholic. A good season means bags full of kumis, fat sheep and many visitors, and songs late into the short nights.",
  "cumans:ritual": "In 1239 the Cumans sealed an alliance with the Latin emperor Baldwin II (Baudouin II). Jean de Joinville recorded the ritual: the Cuman chiefs and Baldwin's men let blood from their hands into a cup, mixed it with wine and water, and drank it, swearing they were now brothers. Then a dog was cut in two between them, and they swore that whoever broke the oath would be cut down the same way.",
  "cumans:rain": "Imagined: in a dry summer the Cuman clans gather beside a stone statue on a kurgan, the image of an ancestor holding a cup at his belly. They pour kumis over its feet, place meat before it, and ask the ancestors to speak to Tengri for rain. The elders watch the sky to the west. If clouds come in three days, the offering was accepted.",
  "cumans:wind": "Imagined: a winter buran sweeps across the Pontic steppe, driving snow so thick that a rider cannot see his horse's head. The Cuman herds turn their backs to it and drift with the wind; the herders follow, roped together, singing to keep the animals calm. A family may lose half its sheep in one night. When it passes, everyone counts what is left, and goes on.",
  "cumans:fire": "In 1239, fleeing the Mongols, Khan Koten (Kötöny) led some forty thousand Cuman families into Hungary. In 1241 Hungarian nobles, suspecting them of spying for the Mongols, murdered Koten in Pest. Enraged, the Cumans left the kingdom, burning villages behind them as they went south into Bulgaria. Smoke rose along their path. They felt betrayed by the people who had promised them refuge.",
  "cumans:thunder": "Imagined: a summer storm builds over the steppe near the Dnieper. Lightning stabs down, and a Cuman herder throws himself flat beside his horse. The Cumans believed Tengri spoke in thunder, and that a place struck by lightning was touched by heaven. Afterwards the family will bring milk to the scorched grass and leave it there, with a quiet word of respect.",
  "cumans:birth": "In 1262 Elizabeth the Cuman (Kun Erzsébet), daughter of a Cuman chief, gave birth to Ladislaus (László), heir to the Hungarian throne. The Cumans who had settled in Hungary hoped he would be their protector. He was: as king, he dressed like a Cuman, lived among them and loved a Cuman girl, until he was murdered by Cuman assassins in 1290. A mother's hope and a people's hope, held in one child.",
  "cumans:death": "Jean de Joinville describes the burial of a Cuman knight in the 1240s, near Constantinople (Κωνσταντινούπολις). A large pit was dug and the dead man set inside, sitting upright in fine clothes. With him they placed his best horse and his squire, both alive, and gold and silver. The khan gave great gifts so that the dead would arrive in the next world as a rich man. Then they covered the pit and raised a mound above it.",

  // ---------------- Tatars ----------------
  "tatars:hunt": "Imagined: on a frosty morning in the Crimean steppe, a Tatar bey rides out with his hounds and a goshawk. Hares break from the dry grass and the hawk strikes. The men gallop and shout, and the bey's young son brings down his first hare with a bow. Tonight there will be a small feast, and the boy's grandfather will tell him he rides like his ancestors.",
  "tatars:battle": "In October 1552 Ivan IV (Иван IV) of Moscow (Москва) besieged Kazan (Казан), capital of the Tatar khanate. For weeks the defenders under Khan Yadegar Mokhammad held the walls against cannon fire. On 2 October Russian sappers blew mines under the walls and stormed the city. The mosques were destroyed and many of the defenders killed in the streets. Tatar songs still remember the fall of Kazan and the Kul Sharif (Кол Шәриф) mosque.",
  "tatars:gather": "Sabantuy (Сабан туе), the festival of the plough, marks the end of spring sowing among the Volga (Идел) Tatars. Young men ride from house to house collecting gifts: embroidered towels, scarves, eggs. Then the whole village gathers on a meadow for wrestling with belts, horse races, sack races and climbing a greased pole for a prize. The work is done, and everyone hopes the fields will repay it.",
  "tatars:ritual": "Imagined: in a Kazan (Казан) Tatar village, a young man must decide whether to travel to Bukhara (بخارا) to study. He does not go to a fortune-teller. He washes, prays two units of prayer at night, and recites the istikhara (استخارة) supplication: O God, if this is good for my faith and my life, make it easy for me. In the morning he tells his mother he will go, and she begins to pack.",
  "tatars:rain": "In dry summers Tatar villages held yangyr televe (яңгыр теләү), asking for rain. People gathered by a river or on a hill, cooked porridge together in a great cauldron, shared it with everyone, and prayed. Then they splashed water on each other, and sometimes even threw the mullah gently into the river. Everyone laughed, but they looked at the sky all the same, hoping the prayer had been heard.",
  "tatars:wind": "Imagined: a winter buran falls on the Volga (Идел) steppe. In the Tatar village the wind fills the roads with snow up to the windows. Families bring the calves inside, and the grandmother lights the lamp and reads aloud from an old book of stories while the house creaks. In the morning the men dig tunnels to the mosque, and the muezzin calls the dawn prayer as usual.",
  "tatars:fire": "In May 1571 the Crimean Tatar khan Devlet Giray (Devlet Geray) raided all the way to Moscow (Москва). On the day his army arrived, a fire began in the suburbs and, driven by wind, swept through the wooden city. The Kremlin (Кремль)'s powder magazine exploded. Chroniclers said tens of thousands died. For the Crimean Tatars it was revenge for Kazan (Казан) and Astrakhan; for Moscow, a catastrophe it never forgot.",
  "tatars:thunder": "In 922 Ibn Fadlan (ابن فضلان) travelled from Baghdad (بغداد) to the Volga (Идел) Bulgars, ancestors of the Kazan Tatars. He wrote that when lightning struck a house, the Bulgars would not go near it, but left it with everything inside to perish, saying it was a house on which wrath had fallen. He also saw the northern lights, and his hosts told him they were believing and unbelieving jinn fighting in the sky.",
  "tatars:birth": "When a Tatar baby is born, family and neighbours gather for isem kushu (исем кушу), the naming. The mullah takes the child, faces Mecca (مكة), and recites the call to prayer into the right ear and the iqama (إقامة) into the left. Then he whispers the name three times. Guests bring gifts and the mother rests for forty days. Everyone prays the child will be healthy, learned and honest, whatever times it lives in.",
  "tatars:death": "Tatar funerals follow Islamic law and have no music. The body is washed, wrapped in a white shroud, and buried the same day if possible, carried on men's shoulders while they recite prayers. Only men go to the graveyard. At home, the women read Yasin (يس), and on the third, seventh and fortieth days the family gathers for prayers and a meal, remembering the dead and asking forgiveness for them.",

  // ---------------- Golden Horde ----------------
  "golden-horde:hunt": "The Mongols' great hunt, the nerge, was a war exercise. Juvaini (جوینی) describes how the whole army formed a ring many days' ride across and slowly drew it tighter, driving every animal inside toward the centre. No beast could escape and no man could break the line without punishment. At last the khan rode in first to shoot, then the princes, then the soldiers. The Golden Horde khans kept the custom on the Volga (Идел) steppe.",
  "golden-horde:battle": "On 11 April 1241, at Mohi (Muhi) on the Sajo (Sajó) river, Batu Khan (Бат хаан) and Subutai (Сүбээдэй) met the army of King Bela IV (IV. Béla) of Hungary. Batu's men forced the bridge with catapults, while Subutai crossed downstream in secret. The Hungarians, trapped in their wagon camp, were cut down as they fled. Batu mourned Bahadur, one of his commanders, killed at the bridge. Within weeks the Horde stood on the Danube.",
  "golden-horde:gather": "Ibn Battuta (ابن بطوطة) visited the camp of Khan Ozbeg (Özbeg) in 1334 and wrote that it was a moving city: wagons with felt houses on top, mosques and markets travelling with it, smoke rising from kitchens as it rolled. At Sarai on the Volga, merchants from Egypt, Genoa (Genova), Persia and Russia gathered. In the camp, the khatuns hosted feasts with kumis served in gold and silver cups.",
  "golden-horde:ritual": "William of Rubruck, a friar sent to the Mongols in 1253, describes how the khans asked about the future. A sheep's shoulder blade was placed in the fire until it cracked from the heat. Then it was brought to the khan, who read the cracks: if they ran straight, the plan would succeed; if crooked, it would fail. No great undertaking began without it, before the khans of the Horde turned to Islam.",
  "golden-horde:rain": "Imagined: in a dry year on the Volga steppe, the grass is short and the herds thin. In Sarai, Khan Ozbeg (Özbeg)'s people go out after Friday prayer for the istisqa (استسقاء), the prayer for rain. Out in the grasslands, the herding families still pour milk to the sky as their grandparents did. Both wait together, Muslim and old believer, for the clouds that will decide the winter.",
  "golden-horde:wind": "Imagined: a buran strikes the Horde's winter camp on the lower Volga (Идел). Snow and wind blow for three days. The felt tents are weighted with stones, and the wagons drawn into a ring around them. Herders work in shifts to keep the horses moving so they do not freeze. When the wind falls, the khan sends riders to every camp to count the losses and share food.",
  "golden-horde:fire": "When John of Plano Carpini (Giovanni da Pian del Carpine), envoy of the Pope, reached Batu (Бат)'s camp in 1246, he was told he must pass between two fires before seeing the khan, along with his gifts. The Mongols believed fire cleansed visitors of any poison or evil they might carry. Two spears stood beside the fires with a rope between them, hung with strips of cloth. Then he was led into Batu's golden tent.",
  "golden-horde:thunder": "Plano Carpini (Pian del Carpine) and Rubruck both noticed that the Mongols, who feared nothing in battle, were terribly afraid of thunder. During a storm they drove strangers out of their tents, wrapped themselves in black felt, and hid until it passed. If someone was struck by lightning, his family had to pass between fires to be purified, and nobody touched the things in his tent.",
  "golden-horde:birth": "Jochi (Зүчи), the eldest son of Genghis Khan (Чингис хаан) and father of Batu (Бат), was born around 1182, soon after his mother Borte (Бөртэ) was rescued from the Merkits (Мэргид) who had captured her. Some whispered he was not Genghis's son. Genghis accepted him, but his brother Chagatai (Цагаадай) once called him a Merkit bastard in front of their father. Jochi's line went on to found the Golden Horde, the longest-lasting of the Mongol khanates.",
  "golden-horde:death": "Plano Carpini (Pian del Carpine) describes the burial of a Mongol noble. He was placed in a tent seated at a table, with a bowl of meat and a cup of mare's milk, and buried with a mare and her foal and a saddled horse, so that he would have milk and a mount in the next world. Another horse was eaten, and its skin stuffed and raised on poles over the grave. Then the place was hidden.",

  // ---------------- Berbers (Amazigh, Tuareg) ----------------
  "berbers:hunt": "On the rock walls of Tassili n'Ajjer (ⵜⴰⵙⵉⵍⵉ ⵏ ⴰⵊⵊⴻⵔ), deep in today's Sahara, painters thousands of years ago drew hunters with bows chasing giraffes and antelope across what was then green savanna. Tuareg herders still camp beneath those paintings. Imagined: a Tuareg hunter tracks a Barbary sheep among the rocks of the Hoggar (ⴰⵀⴰⴳⴳⴰⵔ) at dawn, moving barefoot and silent, as his grandfather taught him, knowing every animal killed must be shared.",
  "berbers:battle": "Around 698, the Amazigh queen Dihya (ⴷⵉⵀⵢⴰ), whom the Arabs called al-Kahina (الكاهنة), the prophetess, led the tribes of the Aures (ⴰⵡⵔⴰⵙ) mountains against the Umayyad general Hassan ibn al-Nu'man (حسان بن النعمان). At the river Meskiana she routed his army and drove it out of Ifriqiya (إفريقية). She treated the captives well and even adopted one of them, Khalid, as her son. For five years the Aures remained free.",
  "berbers:gather": "In the southwest of Morocco, Amazigh women gather the fallen fruit of the argan (ⴰⵔⴳⴰⵏ) tree, found almost nowhere else on earth. Goats climb into the branches to eat the leaves. The women sit together, cracking each hard nut between two stones, one by one, talking and singing, then roast and grind the kernels by hand to press out the oil. A day's work makes barely a litre.",
  "berbers:ritual": "Herodotus (Ἡρόδοτος) wrote that the Nasamones (Νασαμῶνες) of Libya, ancestors of Amazigh peoples, asked their dead for guidance by sleeping on the tombs of their forefathers and taking the dream as an answer. Centuries later, as Muslims, Amazigh villagers bring their questions to God in istikhara (استخارة) prayer and to the shrines of local saints, but the old respect for the ancestors who lie under the stones still runs deep.",
  "berbers:rain": "In dry years, Amazigh villages carried out the taghonja (ⵜⴰⵖⵓⵏⵊⴰ): girls dressed a large wooden ladle as a bride and carried it from house to house singing to Anzar (ⴰⵏⵥⴰⵔ), the rain. The old legend says Anzar loved a girl and wanted her as his bride; when she agreed, the rain came back to the fields. Women poured water over the doll, and everyone watched the Atlas peaks for clouds.",
  "berbers:wind": "In the 19th century travellers reported that in 1805 a great caravan crossing from Timbuktu toward the salt mines of the north, two thousand people and nearly as many camels, was caught by the simoom (سموم), the poison wind, and the wells were dry. Not one survived. Tuareg guides, the masters of that desert, read the sky and the sand with care because they knew how quickly the wind could kill.",
  "berbers:fire": "Arab chroniclers tell that when Dihya (ⴷⵉⵀⵢⴰ), al-Kahina (الكاهنة), knew the Umayyad armies would return, she ordered her people to burn the towns and cut down the orchards from Tripoli (طرابلس) to Tangier (طنجة), so that invaders would find nothing worth taking. Smoke rose over the olive groves. Many of her own people turned against her for it. She fought on, and fell around 703, at a well later called Bir al-Kahina (بئر الكاهنة).",
  "berbers:thunder": "Imagined: in the High Atlas (ⴰⴷⵔⴰⵔ ⵏ ⴷⵔⵏ) a summer storm breaks over a village of red earth houses. Thunder echoes from cliff to cliff, and the women hurry to bring in the barley drying on the rooftops. The children count the flashes. The old man at the mosque door says the storm is a mercy as long as it does not bring hail, and murmurs a prayer for the terraces below.",
  "berbers:birth": "Imagined: in a Tuareg camp a young woman gives birth in her mother's tent, as custom requires for a first child. The women surround her, singing softly, and the midwife holds her hands. On the seventh day comes the naming: a ram is slaughtered, the marabout whispers the name into the baby's ear, and the camp celebrates with tende (ⵜⴻⵏⴷⴻ) drumming and camel races. No one knows what this child will see.",
  "berbers:death": "In a Tuareg legend, Tin Hinan (ⵜⵉⵏ ⵀⵉⵏⴰⵏ) was the queen and ancestress of all the Tuareg. At Abalessa in the Hoggar (ⴰⵀⴰⴳⴳⴰⵔ) archaeologists found a tomb from about the 4th century where a woman lay on a carved bed, wearing gold and silver bracelets. Today Amazigh funerals follow Islam: the dead are washed, shrouded and buried the same day with prayers, and there is no music, only silence and the Quran (القرآن).",

  // ---------------- Ethiopians ----------------
  "ethiopians:hunt": "Imagined: in the highlands near Gondar (ጎንደር) in the 17th century, an Ethiopian noble rides out with his men and their spears after a buffalo that has been raiding the fields. The hunters move through the tall grass, calling to each other in short, sharp shouts. When the buffalo charges, a young man stands his ground. That night the village sings his praises, and his name will be remembered in fukera (ፉከራ), the boasting songs.",
  "ethiopians:battle": "On 1 March 1896, at Adwa (ዓድዋ), the army of Emperor Menelik II (ምኒልክ) and Empress Taytu Betul (ጣይቱ ብጡል) destroyed an invading Italian force. Some hundred thousand Ethiopians, marching from every province, had answered Menelik's call. The Italians lost thousands, and Ethiopia remained independent while nearly all of Africa was colonised. Adwa is still celebrated each year as a victory for every African people.",
  "ethiopians:gather": "Ethiopians tell of Kaldi, a goatherd of Kaffa (ከፋ) who saw his goats dancing after eating red berries from a shrub. Coffee, buna (ቡና), still grows wild in those forests. At the coffee ceremony a woman roasts the green beans over coals, carries the smoking pan around so everyone can smell it, grinds them by hand, and serves three rounds: abol (አቦል), tona (ቶና) and baraka (በረካ), the last one a blessing.",
  "ethiopians:ritual": "On the eve of Timkat (ጥምቀት), Epiphany, priests carry the tabot (ታቦት), the sacred tablet of each church, wrapped in brocade, to a pool or stream. Crowds in white keep vigil through the night. At dawn the water is blessed and sprinkled over everyone. Then priests in embroidered robes sway and chant, shaking the tsenatsil (ጸናጽል) sistrum to the slow beat of the kebero (ከበሮ) drum. It is prayer as a whole people, asking blessing for the year.",
  "ethiopians:rain": "In the Ethiopian highlands, everything waits for kiremt (ክረምት), the great rains from June to September. When they come, the roads turn to mud, rivers rise, and the hills turn green. Farmers plough with oxen and sow teff (ጤፍ). If the rains are late, priests lead processions and the people pray. In the famine years of 1888 to 1892 the rains failed; Ethiopians still call it Kifu Qen (ክፉ ቀን), the evil days.",
  "ethiopians:flood": "The Blue Nile (ዓባይ) rises from Lake Tana (ጣና ሐይቅ) and carries most of the summer flood that once made Egypt fertile. Ethiopian rulers knew it. Several medieval Ethiopian kings were said to have threatened the sultans of Cairo that they could divert the Nile if Christians in Egypt were mistreated. Whether it was ever possible, the flood that Egypt celebrated began as Ethiopian rain on Ethiopian hills.",
  "ethiopians:wind": "Imagined: in the Danakil Depression, one of the hottest places on earth, an Afar (Qafar) salt caravan of a hundred camels is caught by a dust storm. The men cover their faces with cloth and make the camels kneel in a circle. The salt slabs, cut by hand from the salt flats, are tied tight. For hours there is only sand and heat. Then the wind dies, and they march on toward the highlands.",
  "ethiopians:fire": "On Meskel (መስቀል), 27 September, Ethiopians build a tall pyramid of branches tied with yellow Meskel daisies, the demera (ደመራ). It recalls the story of Empress Helena, who lit a fire and followed its smoke to find the buried True Cross. At dusk the demera is lit amid singing and dancing, and when it falls, people read the direction of the collapse as a sign for the coming year. The rains are over; the flowers are out.",
  "ethiopians:thunder": "Imagined: a kiremt (ክረምት) storm breaks over the highlands of Gojjam (ጎጃም). Lightning plays on the round thatched roofs and the thunder rolls down the gorge of the Blue Nile (ዓባይ). In a round church on a hill, the debtera (ደብተራ) keep chanting the night prayers by candlelight, their voices low and steady. The village children listen through the walls and feel safe, because the chant does not stop for the storm.",
  "ethiopians:birth": "In the 12th century, a son was born to the Zagwe (ዛጔ) royal family at Roha (ሮሓ). Tradition says a swarm of bees surrounded the baby without stinging him, and his mother took it as a sign of kingship, naming him Lalibela (ላሊበላ), the bees recognise his sovereignty. His brother feared him and tried to poison him. Lalibela lived, became king, and had eleven churches carved from solid rock, a new Jerusalem (ኢየሩሳሌም) in Ethiopia.",
  "ethiopians:death": "When an Ethiopian dies, the wailing begins at once; neighbours come running. Women mourners, the alqash (አልቃሽ), improvise laments praising the dead, sometimes beating their chests. The priest leads the funeral at the church and the body is buried in the churchyard. Then come days of lekso (ለቅሶ), mourning, when visitors sit with the family in a tent. On the fortieth day, the tazkar (ተዝካር), a memorial feast is held.",

  // ---------------- Malians (Mali Empire, Mande) ----------------
  "malians:hunt": "Among the Mande, hunters belong to the donso ton (donsoton), a brotherhood with its own secrets, protective amulets and its own musicians, who play the donso ngoni harp and sing the deeds of great hunters. The epic of Sundiata (ߛߎ߲ߖߊߕߊ) begins with them: it was a hunter-seer who came to the king of Manden and told him to marry an ugly, hunchbacked woman, Sogolon, for she would bear him a mighty son.",
  "malians:battle": "Around 1235, at Kirina, Sundiata Keita (Sunjata Keyita) met Sumanguru Kante (Sumaworo Kantɛ), the sorcerer-king of Sosso (Soso), who had conquered Manden. The griots say no iron could harm Sumanguru, until Sundiata's sister Nana Triban, who had been given to Sumanguru as a wife, learned his secret: a white rooster's spur. Sundiata's arrow, tipped with it, struck him, and his power broke. The Mali Empire was born on that field.",
  "malians:gather": "The wealth of Mali came from the gold fields of Bambuk and Bure, near the upper Niger (Jɔliba). In the dry season, farmers turned miners dug narrow pits and women washed the earth in calabashes, swirling until the gold grains shone. Mali's kings let the people keep the gold dust but claimed every nugget. Arab writers marvelled; on his hajj Mansa Musa (منسا موسى) spent so much gold that its price in Cairo (القاهرة) fell for years.",
  "malians:ritual": "At Kangaba, every seven years, the Kamablon (Kamabolon), the sacred round house of the Keita, gets a new thatched roof. Men of the village climb up and replace the old straw in one day while crowds watch. Then griots of the Diabate (Jabate) family from Kela, who keep the oral history, recite the story of Sundiata (ߛߎ߲ߖߊߕߊ) and the founding of Mali inside the house, a ceremony said to go back to the 14th century.",
  "malians:rain": "Imagined: in the Sahel near Niani the land has been dry for seven months, fields cracked and grey. At the end of May the first wind brings the smell of rain. People stand outside their compounds watching the clouds build over the south. When the first heavy drops fall, children run naked into it, laughing, and the old farmers kneel to touch the earth, ready to sow millet.",
  "malians:flood": "Each year the Niger (Jɔliba) spreads across its inland delta like a sea. When the water falls, Fulani (Fulɓe) herders bring their cattle back across the river at Diafarabe in the Dewgal, the great crossing. Herders swim with their herds through the current, and the whole town gathers to watch. The best-fed herds are praised in song, and the young men who brought them home are honoured. The river gives life twice a year.",
  "malians:wind": "Imagined: in December the harmattan blows south from the Sahara over Timbuktu (تنبكت). Fine dust hangs in the air, and the sun becomes a pale disc. In the Sankore mosque, scholars wrap their manuscripts in leather and close the wooden chests. Students cover their mouths and keep reciting. For three months the wind dries everything, but the cold nights are clear, and the stars are sharp over the dunes.",
  "malians:fire": "In January 2013, as armed groups retreated from Timbuktu (تنبكت), they set fire to the Ahmed Baba (أحمد بابا) Institute, burning some of the old manuscripts kept there. But librarians, led by Abdel Kader Haidara, had already secretly smuggled hundreds of thousands of manuscripts out of the city in rice sacks and trunks, on donkey carts and river boats, to Bamako (Bamakɔ). The fire took pages; the people saved centuries.",
  "malians:thunder": "Imagined: a storm breaks over a Mande village at the start of the rains. Thunder rolls and lightning strikes a tall baobab at the edge of the fields. The blacksmith, whose craft is believed to command hidden powers, goes out with the elders to look at the tree. They say the storm has chosen it, and no one will cut it. The children whisper that the sky has marked their village.",
  "malians:birth": "The griots tell that Sogolon gave birth to Sundiata (ߛߎ߲ߖߊߕߊ) in a storm, and that the king's other wife, Sassouma, mocked the child, who could not walk until he was seven years old. Then one day, his mother humiliated by Sassouma, Sundiata called for an iron bar, hauled himself upright on it until the bar bent like a bow, and stood. The griots sing that the whole of Manden was born with him.",
  "malians:death": "Mande Muslims bury their dead quickly, the same day if they can. The body is washed, wrapped in white, and carried to the grave while men recite prayers; there is no music at the burial. Women mourn at home. In the days after, relatives come to greet the family, and in many families a griot, jeli (ߖߋߟߌ), recalls the ancestors of the dead, so that the name and the lineage are not forgotten.",

  // ---------------- Hausa ----------------
  "hausa:hunt": "Imagined: in the dry season after the harvest, the hunters of a Hausa village gather under their leader, the sarkin baka, master of the bow. They carry bows, spears and charms and set out with dogs into the bush, beating it in a long line to drive out antelope and guinea fowl. At night they sing the songs of hunters by the fire, and the meat is shared with every compound.",
  "hausa:battle": "In the 16th century, says the Kano Chronicle, Amina, queen of Zazzau, led the armies of Zaria for decades, conquering lands as far as Nupe and Kwararafa, and the rulers paid her tribute. She is remembered for the earthen walls, ganuwar Amina, raised around her war camps and the towns of Hausaland (Ƙasar Hausa). Hausa girls are still told: be like Amina, daughter of Bakwa Turunku.",
  "hausa:gather": "At Kofar Mata (Ƙofar Mata) in Kano, the dye pits have been worked since 1498, by the city's own account. Dyers stir cloth into deep pits of indigo, fermented with ash and potash, and lift it out again and again until it is almost black. Then it is beaten with wooden mallets until it shines. Traders from across the Sahara came for Kano cloth, and the dyers' families still work the same pits.",
  "hausa:ritual": "At the end of Ramadan and at the Feast of Sacrifice (Babbar Sallah), after the Eid prayer, the emirs of Kano, Katsina and Zaria hold the durbar. Horsemen in quilted armour and bright robes charge in groups toward the emir, reining in at the last moment to salute him, while kakaki trumpets blow and drums beat. It began as a review of the cavalry; it is now a prayer of thanks and a promise of loyalty.",
  "hausa:rain": "Imagined: in May the farmers of Katsina look to the sky every evening. The ground has been hard for months. When the first storm is seen in the south, the imam leads the prayer for rain and people give alms. Then the clouds come, and with them a rush to the fields: every hand, women and children too, plants millet and sorghum before the soil dries again.",
  "hausa:flood": "Imagined: in a year of heavy rains the Hadejia (Haɗeja) river breaks over its banks and spreads into the wetlands. Houses of mud brick soften and collapse; families carry what they can to higher ground. The village head organises shelter in the mosque, and neighbours from dry villages bring grain. When the water goes down, the land is rich again, and the rice will grow well next year.",
  "hausa:wind": "Imagined: the harmattan arrives in Kano in November, and soon the dust is so thick that the minarets disappear. Traders cover their goods in Kurmi market with mats, camels kneel by the city gates, and children wrap their faces in their mothers' wrappers. For months the cold dry wind cracks lips and skin. Then one day in February it stops, and the sky is suddenly blue.",
  "hausa:fire": "Imagined: in the dry season, when the harmattan blows, a cooking fire in a Hausa town catches a thatched roof. The flames jump from compound to compound. Men form lines from the wells with clay pots, women carry the children out, and the emir's servants pull down roofs to make a break. By evening a whole ward is ash. The next day neighbours come with grain, mats and cloth.",
  "hausa:thunder": "Imagined: the first great storm of the rainy season comes to a Hausa village at night. Thunder shakes the clay walls and lightning lights the granaries like day. The old women say the spirits of the old bori religion are dancing in the sky; the young imam says it is God's power. Everyone agrees on one thing: the rain is good, and tomorrow the planting begins.",
  "hausa:birth": "When a Hausa baby is born, the mother rests and is cared for in her own family's house, and visitors come with gifts. On the seventh day comes the suna, the naming: a ram is slaughtered at dawn, the imam whispers the name into the baby's ear, and friends crowd the compound with food and drumming. Everyone prays the child will be blessed, learned, and a credit to the family.",
  "hausa:death": "A Hausa Muslim funeral is simple and quick, and has no music. The body is washed, wrapped in white cloth and carried to the cemetery on a bier, usually the same day, while men recite prayers. Women stay at home. For days the family receives ta'aziyya, condolence visits, where friends sit with them and say: may God have mercy on the dead, may He give you patience.",

  // ================ Eleven more scenes per people ================
  // ---------------- Persians: more scenes ----------------
  "persians:siege": "In 359 CE Shapur II (شاپور) brought his army before Amida (ܐܡܝܕ) on the upper Tigris (دجله). On the first day Grumbates, an allied king riding with the Persians, lost his young son to an arrow from the walls. The whole camp mourned him for seven days with songs and dances around the body, then burned it. Only after the mourning did the assault begin. For seventy-three days the soldiers built mounds and towers against the walls, carrying their grief into the last attack.",
  "persians:triumph": "In 588 CE the general Bahram Chobin (بهرام چوبین), with a small army, routed a great host from the eastern steppe near Herat (هرات). The Shahnameh (شاهنامه) tells how the spoils came west in long caravans: gold, horses, tents of silk. In Ctesiphon (تیسفون) the people lined the roads, drums and karnay (کرنای) horns sounded from the gates, and minstrels made songs of the battle. Soldiers came home to their villages as heroes, and for a season every tavern sang of Bahram.",
  "persians:build": "At Shushtar (شوشتر) in the 3rd century, thousands of workers raised the Band-e Kaisar (بند قیصر), a bridge and weir of cut stone across the Karun (کارون), more than five hundred metres long. Masons squared blocks and clamped them with iron and lead; others dug channels to turn the river onto the fields of Khuzestan (خوزستان). Workers sang to keep their hammers together, and for some fourteen hundred years the weir fed mills and gardens, until floods finally broke it in the 19th century.",
  "persians:voyage": "The Qissa-i Sanjan (قصه سنجان) tells how Zoroastrian families left Iran by sea from Hormuz (هرمز), carrying their sacred fire. A storm rose on the way to India, and they vowed that if they lived they would light a great fire of Bahram (آتش بهرام). They reached the coast of Gujarat (ગુજરાત). The local ruler, the story says, sent them a full bowl of milk; their elder stirred in sugar and sent it back. We will sweeten your land, not overflow it.",
  "persians:festival": "Nowruz (نوروز), the new year, begins at the spring equinox. On the last Wednesday night before it, Chaharshanbe Suri (چهارشنبه‌سوری), Persians jump over bonfires singing: give me your red, take my yellow, my pallor. Then the house is swept, the haft-sin (هفت‌سین) table set with seven things whose names begin with s: sprouts, apples, garlic, vinegar, sumac. Families visit elders for thirteen days, and on the thirteenth everyone picnics outdoors and throws the sprouts into running water.",
  "persians:games": "Chovgan (چوگان), polo, is old in Iran. In the Shahnameh (شاهنامه) the young prince Siyavash (سیاوش) plays with his companions before a foreign king, strikes the ball so high it seems to touch the moon, and wins the hall's applause. Centuries later the stone goalposts still stand in the great maydan (میدان) of Isfahan (اصفهان), where horsemen rode before the palace while drums and horns played. For Persians the game was a test of horse, hand and grace at once.",
  "persians:drought": "From 1870 to 1872 the rains failed across Iran. Wheat withered, prices rose beyond reach, and in towns like Mashhad (مشهد) and Isfahan (اصفهان) people ate grass, roots and the bark of trees. Travellers wrote of villages left empty and families walking toward cities where there was no bread either. Ordinary people shared what they had; bakers were mobbed; pious donors set up soup kitchens at shrines. Grandparents spoke of those years for generations, quietly.",
  "persians:quake": "The poet and traveller Nasir Khusraw (ناصر خسرو) reached Tabriz (تبریز) in 1046 and wrote down what its people told him. Four years before, on a night in 1042, the ground had shaken and much of the city fell; some forty thousand died. He found it already rebuilt, its bazaars busy, and he sat with the young poet Qatran (قطران), who had mourned his city in verse. Persians rebuilt, and kept their poems as the memory.",
  "persians:winter": "Imagined: snow closes the road to a village in Azerbaijan for weeks. The family lives around the korsi (کرسی), a low table over a brazier of glowing charcoal, covered with a thick quilt, everyone's legs warm beneath it. Grandfather reads from the Shahnameh (شاهنامه) by lamplight; the children crack walnuts and listen to Rostam (رستم) fight the White Demon (دیو سپید). Outside, wolves call. Inside, they say: winter is long, but the stories are longer.",
  "persians:plague": "In 628 CE, months after Kavad II (قباد) took the throne, plague swept through the Sasanian lands, so deadly that chroniclers named it the plague of Shiroe (شیرویه). The young king himself died of it after reigning less than a year. Whole households in Ctesiphon (تیسفون) fell silent; fires in the temples were kept by whoever was left. Survivors buried what they could and prayed to Ahura Mazda (اهورامزدا) that the Lie would lose its hold on the world.",
  "persians:wedding": "At a Persian aqd (عقد), the bride and groom sit before the sofreh (سفره), a cloth spread with a mirror, candles, honey, eggs and sweets. Women hold a fine cloth over the couple and rub two sugar cones together so life will be sweet. The officiant asks the bride three times if she consents; the guests call out that she has gone to pick flowers, then to fetch rosewater. At last she says yes, and the room bursts into kel (کل), ululation.",

  // ---------------- Old Persian: more scenes ----------------
  "achaemenids:siege": "When Babylon (𒆍𒀭𒊏𒆠) rose against Darius (𐎭𐎠𐎼𐎹𐎢𐎺𐎢𐏁) around 521 BCE, Herodotus (Ἡρόδοτος) says the Old Persian army sat before its walls for nineteen months. The Babylonians mocked them from the battlements: you will take the city when mules bear foals. Then one of Zopyrus's own mules foaled. Zopyrus cut off his nose and ears, fled to the city as a deserter, won its trust, and opened the gates. Darius said he would rather have Zopyrus whole than twenty Babylons.",
  "achaemenids:triumph": "Imagined: soldiers come home to a village in Parsa (𐎱𐎠𐎼𐎿) after a long campaign. Their names are already on clay tablets at Persepolis: so much barley, so much wine for each returning man. Herodotus (Ἡρόδοτος) says the Old Persian ideal was simple: be brave in war, and father many sons; the king sent gifts each year to whoever had most. Tonight the village slaughters a sheep, a minstrel sings of the march, and mothers count their sons twice.",
  "achaemenids:build": "Darius's foundation charter from Susa (𐏁𐎢𐏁𐎠) lists who built his palace: Babylonians baked the bricks, cedar came from Lebanon, gold from Sardis and Bactria, lapis and carnelian from Sogdiana. Ionian and Sardian stonecutters dressed the stone, Medes (𐎶𐎠𐎭) and Egyptians worked the gold and the reliefs. The Persepolis (𐎱𐎠𐎼𐎿) tablets show the workers paid in barley, wine and sheep, and women given extra rations after giving birth. Darius wrote: what had been ordered, that was done.",
  "achaemenids:voyage": "Herodotus (Ἡρόδοτος) says Darius (𐎭𐎠𐎼𐎹𐎢𐎺𐎢𐏁) wanted to know where the Indus met the sea. He sent Scylax of Caryanda (Σκύλαξ ὁ Καρυανδεύς) and others down the river in ships, around 515 BCE. They sailed east, then out into the ocean, and turned west along the coasts of Arabia; in the thirtieth month they reached the Red Sea near Egypt. For Old Persian clerks it was a map drawn in months; for the sailors, a long homesickness ended at last.",
  "achaemenids:festival": "At Mithrakana, the autumn feast of Mithra, the god of contracts and the morning light, the Old Persian court gave gifts and feasted. Duris of Samos (Δοῦρις ὁ Σάμιος), as quoted by Athenaeus (Ἀθήναιος), wrote that this was the one day of the year when the Great King was allowed to get drunk and dance the Persian dance before everyone. Imagine the hall at Persepolis (𐎱𐎠𐎼𐎿): horns and harps, the king stepping out among his nobles, laughter rising to the cedar roof.",
  "achaemenids:games": "Herodotus (Ἡρόδοτος) says Old Persian boys, from five years old to twenty, were taught three things only: to ride, to shoot the bow, and to tell the truth. Xenophon (Ξενοφῶν) describes their contests: races on horseback, javelin throwing at a gallop, hunting together under the eyes of elders who praised the best. A boy who hit the mark while riding hard heard his name called aloud, and his family remembered the day.",
  "achaemenids:drought": "Iran's plateau is dry, and Old Persian farmers lived by water carried underground. Polybius (Πολύβιος) wrote that the Persian kings granted five generations the use of any land to whoever first brought water to it through qanats (قنات), tunnels dug for kilometres from the foot of the mountains. In a drought year, the families kept watching the trickle at the qanat's mouth. While it flowed, the village lived; when it slowed, everyone went quiet.",
  "achaemenids:quake": "Herodotus (Ἡρόδοτος) writes that in 490 BCE, as the Old Persian fleet under Datis sailed past the sacred island of Delos (Δῆλος), the island shook for the first and only time in memory. Datis had spared it, burning incense on the altar out of respect. The sailors, looking back from their decks, saw the temples tremble in the sea. The Greeks took it as a sign of troubles to come; the sailors only rowed faster.",
  "achaemenids:winter": "In Aeschylus (Αἰσχύλος)'s play The Persians (Πέρσαι), a messenger tells of the Old Persian army's retreat from Greece in 480 BCE. Winter came early; one night the river Strymon (Στρυμών) froze, and men who had never prayed to the gods of the earth knelt and gave thanks, then began to cross on the ice. When the sun rose and warmed it, the ice gave way beneath them. Those who drowned soonest, the messenger says, were the lucky ones.",
  "achaemenids:plague": "Herodotus (Ἡρόδοτος) tells that on the long march home from Greece in 480 BCE, the Old Persian army ran out of food. Soldiers ate grass and stripped the bark and leaves from trees; then plague and dysentery broke out among them. Men were left behind in the towns of Thessaly (Θεσσαλία) and Macedonia to be cared for or to die. Those who reached Sardis brought home few songs, and many empty places by the fire.",
  "achaemenids:wedding": "At Susa (𐏁𐎢𐏁𐎠) in 324 BCE, ninety couples were married in a single day, in the Old Persian manner. Chairs were set out in a long row; the grooms sat, drank a toast, and then the brides came in and each sat beside her husband, who took her hand and kissed her. Arrian (Ἀρριανός) says the bridegrooms then led their brides home. Musicians played through five days of feasting under a great tent held up by gilded columns.",


  // ---------------- Bedouin: more scenes ----------------
  "saracens:siege": "Imagined: raiders have cut a Bedouin clan off from the plain, and the families drive their camels up into a rocky refuge in the hills. The camels kneel in a ring with the women and children inside it; the men hold the narrow way with spears and old matchlocks. Water is measured out by the cupful, the milk dries up, and the children stop crying for it. On the third night the poet recites, softly, verses about their grandfathers who held out here before.",
  "saracens:triumph": "Imagined: riders come back across the gravel plain at dusk, driving camels taken in a raid, and the camp sees their dust long before their faces. Women run out with the zaghruta (زغرودة), the high trilling cry; children climb on the tent ropes to look. That night the coffee pot passes from hand to hand, a sheep is boiled, and the tribe's poet stands up to recite. A deed becomes real only once it lives in a qasida (قصيدة).",
  "saracens:build": "The Bedouin house is the bayt al-sha'r (بيت الشعر), the house of hair. Women spin black goat hair and weave it in long narrow strips on a ground loom staked out in the sand, then sew the strips together. When the camp moves, everyone pitches the tent at once: poles raised, ropes pulled taut and pegged, the woven curtain hung to divide the men's side from the women's. In rain the hair swells and the roof seals itself.",
  "saracens:voyage": "In the 11th century the Banu Hilal (بنو هلال) left Upper Egypt and moved west across North Africa with their herds and households. The epic of their journey, the Taghribat Bani Hilal (تغريبة بني هلال), is still sung by poets in Egypt to the rababa (ربابة), night after night: Abu Zayd (أبو زيد) scouting ahead in disguise, the beautiful Jaziya (الجازية), the long wandering in search of green land. For the Bedouin the migration became a song that never ends.",
  "saracens:festival": "Each year before Islam, the Arabs met at the fair of Ukaz (عكاظ) near Ta'if (الطائف), in the sacred months when fighting was forbidden. Tribes pitched tents, traded leather, perfume and camels, and above all listened to poets. Tradition says the finest odes were honoured as the Mu'allaqat (المعلقات), the hanging poems. A Bedouin poet who stood up at Ukaz and held the crowd brought glory home to his whole tribe for a generation.",
  "saracens:games": "Bedouin storytellers remember the race of Dahis (داحس) and al-Ghabra (الغبراء) in the 6th century: two famous horses, one from the tribe of Abs (عبس) and one from Dhubyan (ذبيان), matched over a long desert course for a stake of camels. Men from one side hid in a valley and frightened the leading horse. The quarrel over who had truly won became a war of forty years. Afterwards, old men said: never bet your tribe on a horse.",
  "saracens:drought": "Imagined: the winter rains have failed twice, and the wells of the tribe's dira (ديرة), its home range, give only mud. The camels' humps sag; the goats are slaughtered one by one before they starve. The elders ride out for days to ask a distant tribe for the right to water at their wells, offering their oldest bond of kinship. At night an old man recites verses about the lightning that flickers far away, over someone else's land.",
  "saracens:quake": "In 1256 the ground near Medina (المدينة) shook for days, and then the black lava field of Harrat Rahat (حرة رهط) split open. Chroniclers wrote that fire lit the night so brightly that people in Medina could read by it, and that the glow was seen from far across the desert. A river of molten rock crept north toward the town and stopped short of it. Bedouin herders watched from the hills, praying and afraid.",
  "saracens:plague": "In 639 CE plague broke out at Amwas (عمواس) near Jerusalem (القدس) among Arab soldiers and their families, many of them men of the desert tribes. Tens of thousands died, including the commander Abu Ubayda (أبو عبيدة). The tradition says that when the caliph turned back rather than enter the stricken land, someone asked: are you fleeing from God's decree? He answered: we flee from God's decree to God's decree. Survivors went home to the tents with the news.",
  "saracens:wedding": "Imagined: at a Bedouin wedding the bride rides to her new tent in a hawdaj (هودج), a curtained litter swaying on a camel, while her kinswomen sing beside it. The men stand in a long line and chant the sahja (سحجة), clapping in answer to the poet who improvises verses about the groom. Guns are fired into the air, a sheep is cooked with rice and yoghurt, and the new couple's tent stands a little apart from the camp.",

  // ---------------- Arab: more scenes ----------------
  "abbasids:siege": "In 812 and 813 Baghdad بغداد was besieged during a war between two brothers. Catapults struck the Round City, whole quarters burned, and the poor of the markets, called the 'urat, the naked ones, fought in the streets with reed shields and baskets of stones on their heads. Food ran out; families fled across the Tigris دجلة. The poet al-Khuraymi wrote a long lament: Baghdad, once the paradise of the earth, now its people weep.",
  "abbasids:triumph": "Imagined: the frontier troops come home to Baghdad بغداد after a summer campaign. Crowds line the road from the Khurasan Gate; boys climb on the walls, women watch from the rooftops, and the buq horns and naqqara drums answer one another. In the evening the markets stay open late, lamps burning, and in the courtyards singers with the oud عود perform new verses about the campaign. Mothers who see their sons ride by give alms on the spot.",
  "abbasids:build": "Between 848 and 852 the caliph al-Mutawakkil built the Great Mosque of Samarra سامراء, for a time the largest mosque in the world. Thousands of Arab and other workers fired bricks, raised walls, and built its minaret, al-Malwiya الملوية, a spiral tower fifty metres high with a ramp winding around the outside. Brickmakers sang as they stacked the kilns. The minaret still stands above the plain of the Tigris.",
  "abbasids:voyage": "In 921 Ahmad ibn Fadlan (أحمد بن فضلان) set out from Baghdad (بغداد) with an embassy to the Volga Bulghars. He crossed Iran and Khwarazm (خوارزم), then the frozen steppe, where he wrote that his beard froze into a block of ice and the cold made men's hands stick to iron. For nearly a year the caravan travelled. When at last they saw the river and the Bulghar king's tents, Ibn Fadlan wrote down everything, curious and astonished.",
  "abbasids:festival": "Imagined: on the last evening of Ramadan (رمضان) in Abbasid Baghdad (بغداد), people climb onto their roofs to watch for the thin new moon. When it is sighted, the cry goes from roof to roof and drums sound in the streets. Next morning everyone wears new clothes, goes out to the Eid (عيد) prayer, and then visits family. Children carry sweets and coins; cooks sell date cakes and qata'if (قطايف); the whole city smells of rosewater.",
  "abbasids:games": "Abu Bakr al-Suli (أبو بكر الصولي) was the greatest chess player of Baghdad (بغداد) in the early 10th century. He played the caliph al-Muktafi (المكتفي)'s champion, al-Mawardi (الماوردي), in the palace and beat him so thoroughly that the caliph, who had favoured the other man, said: your rosewater has turned to urine. Al-Suli could play blindfold, wrote books of chess problems, and his name became a saying: to play like al-Suli.",
  "abbasids:drought": "Imagined: the winter rains fail and the Euphrates (الفرات) canals run low. In an Iraqi town the imam calls the people to salat al-istisqa (صلاة الاستسقاء), the prayer for rain. They walk out of the gates at dawn in old clothes, humble, bringing children and even their animals. After the prayer the imam turns his cloak inside out, a sign that God will turn the drought around. Then everyone looks to the sky for clouds.",
  "abbasids:quake": "In 977 and 978 a series of earthquakes shook Siraf (سيراف), the great Arab and Persian port on the Gulf, for seven days. Siraf's merchants had sent ships to India and China; its houses were of teak, several storeys high. Many fell, and the people fled to the sea. The merchants moved their trade to the island of Kish (كيش), and the old harbour was slowly forgotten.",
  "abbasids:winter": "Imagined: in a rare cold year, snow falls on Baghdad (بغداد) and lies white on the palm trees. Children who have never seen it scoop it into their hands and throw it at one another. In the markets the price of charcoal doubles; the poor wrap themselves in every cloak they own, and the mosques fill with people sleeping by the lamps. Poets write verses about the strange white guest from the north.",
  "abbasids:plague": "In 1348 the Black Death reached Cairo (القاهرة). The historian al-Maqrizi (المقريزي) wrote that in some days thousands died, that coffins ran short and the dead were carried on boards and doors, and that whole streets fell silent. The call to prayer and the chanting of the Qur'an (القرآن) at funerals went on without pause. Craftsmen, scholars, slaves and emirs died alike. Afterwards, Arab scholars wrote treatises of consolation for those who lived.",
  "abbasids:wedding": "In 825 the caliph al-Ma'mun (المأمون) married Buran (بوران), daughter of his vizier, at Fam al-Silh (فم الصلح). Chroniclers say her grandmother poured a thousand great pearls from a golden tray over the couple, and that a candle of ambergris weighing two hundred pounds lit the room. Outside, balls of musk were thrown to the crowd. Ordinary Arab weddings were smaller, but shared the same spirit: perfume, music and generosity until morning.",

  // ---------------- Levantine: more scenes ----------------
  "ayyubids:siege": "During the long siege of Acre (عكا), from 1189 to 1191, a Levantine swimmer named Isa (عيسى) carried letters and money to the defenders at night, swimming under the enemy ships with a belt of gold around his waist. Baha al-Din (بهاء الدين) wrote that one night he did not arrive. Days later his body washed up on the beach, the gold still bound to him. The people of Acre said he had kept his trust even in death.",
  "ayyubids:triumph": "Imagined: soldiers ride back into Damascus (دمشق) through Bab al-Faradis (باب الفراديس) after a long campaign. The tablkhana (طبلخانة) plays at the citadel, the great kus (كوس) drum booming while nafir (نفير) trumpets answer. Women throw rose petals and sugared almonds from the latticed windows; shopkeepers hang carpets from their stalls. In the coffee and sweet shops the hakawati (حكواتي), the storyteller, already weaves the returning men into the old tales of Antar (عنترة).",
  "ayyubids:build": "Under al-Zahir Ghazi (الظاهر غازي), around 1209, Levantine masons rebuilt the citadel of Aleppo (حلب) on its great mound. They faced the slope with a smooth glacis of stone, carved a deep moat, and built the bridge and the high gateway with its bent entrance. Inside they dug cisterns, a mosque and a palace. The stonecutters sang as they hauled each block uphill; their marks can still be found on the walls.",
  "ayyubids:voyage": "Each year the hajj (حج) caravan left Damascus (دمشق) for Mecca (مكة), a journey of more than a month across the desert. The whole city came out to see it go: the mahmal (محمل), a decorated litter on a camel, at its head, drums and pipes playing, pilgrims in their best clothes. Families wept and embraced at the gate; nobody knew who would return. Weeks later, when the caravan came back, the same crowd sang them home.",
  "ayyubids:festival": "Ibn Khallikan (ابن خلكان) described the Mawlid (المولد), the Prophet's birthday, at Irbil (أربيل) in the early 13th century, under the emir Gökböri (كوكبوري). Wooden pavilions of several storeys lined the road, each filled with singers, musicians and shadow players, and people came from Baghdad (بغداد) and Mosul (الموصل) to see them. Torches burned all night, sheep and camels were cooked for the crowds, and Sufis sang until dawn. The Levantine towns celebrated it with candles and sweets.",
  "ayyubids:games": "Ayyubid princes loved polo, and Damascus (دمشق) had its green maydan by the river Barada (بردى) where horsemen played. Baha al-Din (بهاء الدين) wrote that Saladin (صلاح الدين) played polo even on campaign. Imagine the crowd along the edge of the field: the curved sticks, the leather ball, horses wheeling in the dust, the tablkhana drumming every goal. Levantine boys played the same game on foot in the alleys, with a stick and a rag ball.",
  "ayyubids:drought": "In 1200 the Nile (النيل) failed to rise. The physician Abd al-Latif al-Baghdadi (عبد اللطيف البغدادي), who was living in Cairo (القاهرة), wrote of the years that followed: grain vanished from the markets, villages emptied, the poor ate whatever they could find, and the dead lay in the streets. He recorded what he saw so that no one would forget. Caravans of the starving fled toward Syria, and Levantine towns opened their gates to them.",
  "ayyubids:quake": "Before dawn on 20 May 1202, a great earthquake struck the Levant. Nablus (نابلس) was almost flattened; walls fell at Acre (عكا) and Tyre (صور); in Damascus (دمشق) parts of the great mosque collapsed. Abd al-Latif al-Baghdadi (عبد اللطيف البغدادي) wrote that it was felt from Egypt to Armenia. Families slept in the open for weeks, afraid to go back under roofs. Then, slowly, they cleared the stones and began rebuilding.",
  "ayyubids:winter": "Imagined: snow closes the passes of Mount Lebanon (جبل لبنان). In a stone village, families gather round the tannur (تنور) oven, baking thin bread and drinking spiced tea while the wind howls. The elders store wheat, raisins and dried figs from the autumn; there is enough if everyone shares. In the evenings a neighbour plays the oud (عود) and sings the old mawwal (موال). Spring, they tell the children, always comes back down the valley.",
  "ayyubids:plague": "Ibn Battuta (ابن بطوطة) was in Damascus (دمشق) in July 1348 when the Black Death was killing two thousand a day. He wrote that the people fasted three days, then all of them went out together to the mosque of the footprints (مسجد الأقدام): emirs, sharifs, judges, Jews with their Torah (תּוֹרָה), Christians with their Gospels, women and children, barefoot, praying and weeping. He thought God lightened the plague for them after that.",
  "ayyubids:wedding": "A Levantine wedding lasts days. On the henna night, the bride's friends paint her hands and sing to her. Next day comes the zaffa (زفة): drummers and singers lead the groom through the streets, women's zaghrutas rising from every balcony. At the feast the men link arms for the dabke (دبكة), stamping in rhythm while the mijwiz (مجوز) pipe plays, and the bride's trousseau is carried to her new home for all to see.",


  // ---------------- Oghuz: more scenes ----------------
  "turks:siege": "In May 1097 a great army from the west closed around Nicaea (Νίκαια), where the Oghuz sultan Kilij Arslan (قلج ارسلان) had left his family and treasure. For seven weeks the Turkish garrison held the walls by the lake while siege towers crept closer and the heads of fallen comrades were thrown over the walls. When boats appeared on the lake behind them, the defenders knew they were cut off. They surrendered the city quietly, saving the women and children inside.",
  "turks:triumph": "In the Book of Dede Korkut (Kitab-ı Dedem Korkut), when a hero comes home from a hard fight, the Oghuz pitch white pavilions on the green meadow, slaughter stallions, rams and camels, and feast for forty days and forty nights. Then Dede Korkut, the white-bearded bard, arrives with his kopuz, plays, and makes the hero's story into a song: let it be told, he says, let it pass from mouth to mouth. Each tale ends with his blessing.",
  "turks:build": "When an Oghuz family needed new felt for its yurt, the women of the camp gathered to make it together. They laid out wool on a reed mat, sprinkled it with hot water, rolled it tight, and then rolled the bundle back and forth with their feet and arms for hours, singing work songs to keep the rhythm. By evening the felt was firm and thick, ready to cover the yurt for winter. The camp shared a meal afterwards.",
  "turks:voyage": "The Oghuz Name (Oğuz Nâme) tells how Oghuz Khagan (Oğuz Kağan) led his people out of the east. One morning a light shone into his tent, and from it came a great grey wolf, who said: I will walk before your army. The wolf led the herds, wagons and families across rivers and mountains, and wherever it stopped, the people camped. Historians say the Oghuz moved west from the steppes by the Aral Sea in the 10th century; the song remembers the wolf.",
  "turks:festival": "At Nevruz in spring the Oghuz and their descendants remember the Ergenekon legend: their ancestors, trapped for generations in a valley ringed by iron mountains, found a way out when a smith melted the iron cliff with seventy bellows, and a grey wolf led them into the open world. So on the new year day, families jump over bonfires, and a smith strikes red-hot iron on the anvil, to remember the day the mountain opened.",
  "turks:games": "In the Book of Dede Korkut (Kitab-ı Dedem Korkut), Bamsi Beyrek (Bamsı Beyrek) goes to see Banu Chichek (Banu Çiçek), the girl he was promised to, and she challenges him: first they race horses, then they shoot arrows, then they wrestle. Beyrek wins the race and the shooting, and in the wrestling he only just pins her. Then she laughs and accepts him. Oghuz boys and girls played the same games: horse races, archery, wrestling on the grass at every gathering.",
  "turks:drought": "Imagined: the spring rains have failed, and the Oghuz pastures on the plateau are yellow by June. The springs dry up one by one. The whole camp climbs a hill together, men, women and children, with sheep to sacrifice. They pray for rain, then cook the meat and share it with everyone, even the poorest family. The old woman says that whenever people share, the sky remembers them.",
  "turks:quake": "Imagined: in Erzincan, where the ground has shaken many times, an Oghuz shepherd is woken at night by his dogs howling. Then the earth roars; houses of mud brick fold down like tents. By morning the families sit together in the fields, bandaging wounds, counting who is missing. Neighbours bring bread and felt blankets. The old men say the earth is carried on the horns of a great ox, and that it shifts its weight.",
  "turks:winter": "Imagined: the Oghuz camp has moved down to its winter quarters, the kishlak (kışlak), in a sheltered valley. Snow piles against the felt yurts; inside, the dung fire smoulders and the family sleeps under sheepskins with the newborn lambs. Men ride out to break the ice on the stream so the horses can drink. At night a bard plays the kopuz and tells of Salur Kazan, and everyone waits for the first grass.",
  "turks:plague": "Imagined: news comes to an Oghuz camp that sickness is in the towns on the caravan road. The elders decide at once: pack the yurts, drive the herds into the high pastures, meet no one. For weeks the families live apart, sending a rider only to the edge of the next camp to shout the news across. When a child falls ill, the mother sits up with him through the night, singing the songs her mother sang.",
  "turks:wedding": "Among the Oghuz, a wedding was a feast of many days. In the Book of Dede Korkut (Kitab-ı Dedem Korkut), when Bamsi Beyrek (Bamsı Beyrek) finally marries Banu Chichek (Banu Çiçek), a bridal tent is pitched, horses and sheep are slaughtered, and archers shoot at targets while riders race. Beyrek shoots an arrow, and where it falls the bridal tent is raised. Women sing over the bride, the kopuz plays all night, and Dede Korkut sings blessings over the couple.",

  // ---------------- Anatolian Turks: more scenes ----------------
  "ottomans:siege": "In the summer and autumn of 1877, soldiers under Osman Pasha (Osman Paşa) held the town of Plevna (Plevne) for five months against a much larger army. They dug trenches and redoubts, threw back three great assaults, and then starved slowly as winter came. In December, with food gone, they tried to break out and failed. Anatolian Turks still sing the folk song Plevne Marşı, mourning the young men who held out there and never came home.",
  "ottomans:triumph": "When good news reached Istanbul (استانبول), the city held a donanma: for three days and nights every house, shop and mosque hung out oil lamps. Ships in the Golden Horn (Haliç) were lit with lanterns, fireworks rose over the water, and the mehter (مهتر) band played in the squares with zurna and kös. Shadow puppet players, acrobats and storytellers worked the crowds. Anatolian Turks would walk all night just to see the lights.",
  "ottomans:build": "From 1550 to 1557 the architect Sinan (سنان) built the Süleymaniye mosque on Istanbul (استانبول)'s third hill. Its account books survive: thousands of workers, about half Christian and half Muslim, masons from the Balkans, carpenters, glass makers, carvers, each paid by the day. Sinan, it is said, waited years for the foundations to settle before raising the dome. Anatolian Turks still say his mosques seem to float above the city.",
  "ottomans:voyage": "Evliya Çelebi wrote that in 1630 he dreamed he was in a mosque full of the Prophet's companions. He meant to ask for şefaat, intercession, but stammered and said seyahat, travel. The Prophet smiled and granted both. Evliya then spent forty years travelling, from Vienna (Wien) to Egypt, the Caucasus to Arabia, recording songs, foods and dialects in his ten-volume Seyahatname (سیاحتنامه), delighted by everything he saw.",
  "ottomans:festival": "Hıdırellez, on the night of 5 to 6 May, marks the day the prophets Hızır and İlyas meet on earth and summer begins. Anatolian Turks tie wishes written on paper, or a ribbon, to a rose bush, and bury small tokens beneath it. Young people jump over bonfires, girls drop rings into a pot of water to tell fortunes with verses called mani, and families picnic by springs and green meadows.",
  "ottomans:games": "Every summer since at least the 14th century, oil wrestlers have met at Kırkpınar near Edirne (ادرنه). Legend says forty soldiers wrestled there in pairs, and two of them wrestled until both died; a spring rose where they lay. The wrestlers, in leather breeches, cover themselves with olive oil and pray before beginning. The davul and zurna play while the cazgır, the announcer, calls out verses for each pair.",
  "ottomans:drought": "From 1873 to 1875 the rains failed in central Anatolia, and the harvests in Ankara, Konya and Kayseri collapsed. Sheep and cattle died, villages emptied as families walked to the towns, and tens of thousands died of hunger. People prayed for rain in the fields, the imam leading them in the yağmur duası; neighbours who still had grain shared it. Their grandchildren remembered these as the years of the great hunger.",
  "ottomans:quake": "On 10 September 1509, an earthquake struck Istanbul (استانبول) so hard that the people called it Kıyamet-i Suğra (قیامت صغری), the Little Day of Judgement. More than a thousand houses and over a hundred mosques fell, sea waves broke over the walls, and aftershocks went on for weeks. Families camped in gardens and open squares, afraid to sleep under roofs. Thousands of workers were then called from Anatolia to rebuild the city.",
  "ottomans:winter": "In the winter of 1621 the Bosphorus (Boğaziçi) froze from shore to shore. Chroniclers wrote that people walked across the ice from Üsküdar to Istanbul (استانبول), and that boats were locked fast in the Golden Horn. Food could not come by sea, bread grew scarce, and the poor burned what wood they could find. Anatolian Turks remembered it as the winter when the sea itself stood still.",
  "ottomans:plague": "The Flemish envoy Ogier Ghiselin de Busbecq lived in Istanbul (استانبول) in the 1550s and 1560s and saw plague come again and again. He wrote that Anatolian Turks did not flee it, believing each person's time was written on his forehead by God. They washed the dead, prayed over them and buried them quickly, and went on with their lives. When the plague entered his own household, Busbecq too found little else to do but wait.",
  "ottomans:wedding": "An Anatolian Turkish wedding begins with the kına gecesi, the henna night: the bride sits veiled in red while women sing sad songs of leaving her mother's house, and henna is pressed into her palm. Next day the davul and zurna lead the groom's family to fetch her, with a flag carried at the front. After the ceremony, the groom's friends thump him on the back as he goes in to his bride, wishing him luck.",


  // ---------------- Armenians: more scenes ----------------
  "armenians:siege": "In 1064 a Seljuk army surrounded Ani (Անի), the city of a thousand and one churches, on its cliffs above the Akhurian (Ախուրյան) river. For twenty-five days the Armenians on the walls held out while catapults struck and food ran low. When the walls were breached, many died in the streets and churches. The chronicler Aristakes Lastivertsi (Արիստակես Լաստիվերտցի) wrote a lament for Ani: where are the songs of its feasts, the voices of its singers? Now only weeping is heard.",
  "armenians:triumph": "In the Armenian epic David of Sasun (Սասունցի Դավիթ), young David rides out on his fiery horse Jalali (Քուռկիկ Ջալալի) with his father's lightning sword and defeats the giant Msra Melik (Մսրա Մելիք), who had demanded tribute from Sasun. When he rides home, the people of the mountains come down to meet him with bread and wine, the old men weep, and the women sing. Bards sang the tale in villages for a thousand years, and every listener felt like a citizen of Sasun.",
  "armenians:build": "In the 13th and 14th centuries the master Momik (Մոմիկ) carved stone at the monastery of Noravank (Նորավանք): the church of the Holy Mother of God, and khachkars (խաչքարեր), cross-stones, so fine they look like lace. Armenian masons cut the soft red tuff, shaped each block by hand, and fitted them without iron. Villagers hauled the stone up the gorge. On the finished walls the builders sometimes carved their names, asking those who pray there to remember them.",
  "armenians:voyage": "Between 1682 and 1693 Hovhannes Joughayetsi (Հովհաննես Ջուղայեցի), an Armenian merchant of New Julfa (Նոր Ջուղա), travelled from Isfahan (Սպահան) to India and on across the Himalaya to Lhasa (ལྷ་ས), carrying silk, cloth and silver for his partners. He kept an account book in Armenian, writing down every price and every road. Armenian families back home waited years for letters; when a caravan returned, the whole quarter came out to hear the stories.",
  "armenians:festival": "In ancient Armenia, Navasard Նավասարդ, the new year, came in August after the harvest. Movses Khorenatsi tells how people gathered at Bagavan Բագավան, where the king offered sacrifices to the gods, and there were games, songs, dances and contests of horsemen. Pilgrims brought fruit and flowers, and the feast lasted days. Even after the conversion to Christianity, the name Navasard lived on as the first month of the Armenian year.",
  "armenians:games": "At village feasts in Armenia, young men wrestled in kokh (կոխ), the traditional wrestling, on a meadow ringed by spectators. The dhol (դհոլ) drummer and the zurna (զուռնա) player struck up a fast tune for each bout, and the wrestlers circled, gripping belts and shoulders, while the old men shouted advice. The winner was lifted high and given a ram or a cake. Then the music turned into a dance, and everyone joined the circle.",
  "armenians:drought": "In dry years Armenian village children made a doll called Nurin (Նուրին), dressed it in bright cloth, and carried it from house to house singing: Nurin, Nurin, come to us, bring rain, bring bread. At each door the women poured water over the doll and the children and gave them eggs, butter or flour. The children laughed, soaked; their mothers looked up at the cloudless sky and hoped the song would be heard.",
  "armenians:quake": "On 4 June 1679 a great earthquake struck the Ararat (Արարատ) valley. Villages near Yerevan (Երևան) fell, and at Garni (Գառնի) the old pillared temple on its cliff, standing for some sixteen centuries, collapsed into a heap of stones. Armenian families buried their dead and rebuilt their churches. The temple's stones lay where they fell for nearly three hundred years until they were raised again, one by one.",
  "armenians:winter": "Imagined: deep snow covers an Armenian village in the highlands, and the family lives around the tonir թոնիր, the clay oven sunk in the floor. A low table with a thick quilt is set over it, and everyone pushes their feet underneath to keep warm. Grandmother bakes lavash on the tonir's walls in the morning; at night grandfather tells the tales of David of Sasun Սասունցի Դավիթ. Outside the wind blows, and the cows warm the house next door.",
  "armenians:plague": "Imagined: plague has come to a village in the Armenian highlands. The priest rings the church bell for each death until people beg him to stop. Doors are marked, families leave bread at the threshold for the sick inside. In the evening the women gather at the church and sing the sharakan (շարական) hymns softly together. The children are sent up to the summer pastures with grandparents, out of the village, to wait.",
  "armenians:wedding": "At an Armenian wedding, the groom's mother meets the couple at the door of their new home. A piece of thin lavash (լավաշ) bread is laid over each one's shoulders so they will never go hungry, and they each break a plate under their feet for luck. The kavor (քավոր), the godfather, leads the ceremony. Then the dhol (դհոլ) and zurna begin, everyone dances the kochari (քոչարի) in a circle, and the feast goes on until morning.",

  // ---------------- Georgians: more scenes ----------------
  "georgians:siege": "Imagined: in an upper Svaneti (სვანეთი) village, raiders have come up the valley. The families climb into the stone towers that stand beside every house, pull up the ladder, and close the door. Through the narrow windows men shoot arrows; the women have water, cheese and grain stored above. For days they wait, singing low together in three voices to keep their courage. When the raiders leave, the bells ring in the village church.",
  "georgians:triumph": "Imagined: men come back to a Kakhetian village after a long fight, and the women meet them at the edge of the vineyards. That night the supra (სუფრა) is laid: bread, cheese, roasted meat and wine poured from clay qvevri (ქვევრი). The tamada (თამადა) raises toast after toast, to those who returned, to those who did not, to the land and the vine, and the men answer with the old songs, three voices weaving together until dawn.",
  "georgians:build": "Svetitskhoveli (სვეტიცხოველი) cathedral at Mtskheta (მცხეთა) was rebuilt in the 11th century by the master Arsukidze (არსუკიძე). Georgians tell a sad legend about it: the king was so jealous that no one else should ever have such a church that he ordered the master's right hand cut off. Whether it happened or not, on the cathedral wall there is a carved hand holding a square, with the words: the hand of Arsukidze, servant of God.",
  "georgians:voyage": "In the late 10th century Georgian monks led by John (იოანე) and his son Euthymius (ექვთიმე) made the long journey west to Mount Athos (Ἄθως), across the Black Sea and the Aegean. There, around 980, they founded the monastery of Iviron (ივერთა მონასტერი), the monastery of the Georgians. Euthymius spent his life translating books into Georgian so that monks at home could read them. The monks sent letters home, and lit candles for the families they had left behind.",
  "georgians:festival": "Every September, Georgians come from all over Kakheti (კახეთი) to the cathedral of Alaverdi (ალავერდი) for Alaverdoba (ალავერდობა). They camp in the meadows around the old walls, bring lambs and wine, and celebrate for days. There are horse races, wrestling, singing and dancing; people from the mountain villages bring their own songs. In the old days the feast lasted almost a week, and it was the time when the grape harvest began.",
  "georgians:games": "In Guria (გურია), on Easter Sunday, two villages play lelo burti (ლელო ბურთი), a game with a heavy leather ball. The priest blesses the ball, and then hundreds of men, sometimes the whole village, struggle to carry it across a stream into the other side's field. There are no positions and almost no rules. When the winning side brings the ball home, it is laid on the grave of a villager who died that year, as an honour.",
  "georgians:drought": "Imagined: a dry summer in Kakheti კახეთი, and the vines are shrivelling on the slopes. The village goes up to the hilltop chapel of St George, carrying bread, a sheep and wine from last year's qvevri ქვევრი. The priest prays, the men sing a slow hymn in three voices, and everyone eats together on the grass. Walking home in the evening, they look at the sky over the Caucasus for a single cloud.",
  "georgians:quake": "Imagined: in a village in Racha (რაჭა), high in the mountains, a deep rumble comes up from the earth one evening. Stone houses crack, church domes split, rocks slide down onto the roads. Neighbours dig each other out with their hands, by lantern light. In the morning the priest holds a service in the open air, and the survivors sing a funeral chant in three voices that echoes up the valley.",
  "georgians:winter": "Imagined: in winter, snow cuts off the villages of Tusheti (თუშეთი) and Svaneti (სვანეთი) for months. Families gather near the hearth in the stone house, the cows on the ground floor warming the people above. They mend tools, spin wool, and tell stories of St George (წმინდა გიორგი). At night men sing the old work and hunting songs, and grandmothers teach the young ones the bass part. The cold is long, but the songs are always in three voices.",
  "georgians:plague": "Imagined: plague reaches Tbilisi (თბილისი) in summer, and the city empties. Families who can, flee to their villages in the hills; others shut their doors and wait. Priests walk the narrow streets with icons and incense, singing prayers. Those who survive light candles at Sioni (სიონი) cathedral and carve small crosses into the walls. Afterwards the old people say: we lived because we did not forget how to pray together.",
  "georgians:wedding": "A Georgian wedding is a great supra (სუფრა). Guests bring wine and bread; the tamada (თამადა), the toastmaster, leads dozens of toasts through the night: to the couple, to the parents, to the ancestors, to friendship. Between toasts the guests sing in three-part harmony, and young people dance, the man proud and upright, the woman gliding as if her feet do not touch the floor. Old songs called maqruli (მაყრული) once welcomed the bride's procession.",


  // ---------------- Huns: more scenes ----------------
  "huns:siege": "Priscus (Πρίσκος) records that around 467 a band of Huns and Goths was trapped by a Roman army in a hollow in Thrace (Θρᾴκη). Hemmed in, without food, they sent envoys offering to give up if they were granted land to farm. The Romans promised it, and divided them into groups, then turned on them. Priscus tells it as treachery; for the Huns who died there, it was the last stand of hungry families far from their steppe.",
  "huns:triumph": "Priscus (Πρίσκος), an envoy from Constantinople (Κωνσταντινούπολις), saw Attila return to his village on the plain in 449. Young women came out to meet him in rows, walking under long white linen veils held up by other women, singing Hunnic songs. As he passed the house of his minister Onegesius (Ὀνηγήσιος), Onegesius's wife came out with food and wine on a silver tray, and Attila ate from his horse. The whole village was filled with music and joy.",
  "huns:build": "Priscus (Πρίσκος) describes Attila's village on the Hungarian plain in 449: a great wooden house with towers, built of polished planks so smoothly joined they looked like one piece, surrounded by a wooden palisade. Nearby, Onegesius (Ὀνηγήσιος) had a stone bath built by a captive architect from Sirmium, who had hoped to be freed for his work but was made the bath keeper instead. Everything else was of wood, carried from far away by Hun workers.",
  "huns:voyage": "Imagined: around 370 the Huns cross the Volga and move west across the steppe with everything they own. Wagons with felt covers carry the old, the children and the women; herds of horses, cattle and sheep stretch for miles. Each night the camp is pitched in a ring. Roman writers said they appeared suddenly, as if from nowhere. For the families, it is a long, slow road toward grass that no one has yet grazed.",
  "huns:festival": "At Attila's banquet in 449, Priscus (Πρίσκος) saw the guests drink toasts in order of rank, from golden and silver cups. When evening came, torches were lit and two singers stood before Attila and sang songs they had made of his victories and his courage. The guests listened intently; some grew joyful, others wept, the old men longing for battles they could no longer ride to. Then a jester came in, and everyone laughed.",
  "huns:games": "Imagined: on a summer day on the plain, young Huns hold a contest. Roman writers said Huns seemed glued to their horses, eating and even sleeping on them. Riders gallop past a target, turn in the saddle, and loose their arrows backwards; others race to snatch a lance from the ground at full speed. The old warriors judge, shouting and laughing. The best rider gets a horse and a cup of mare's milk.",
  "huns:drought": "Imagined: in the 5th century, tree rings tell us that the Hungarian plain had years of hard drought. Hun families watch the grass wither and the river shrink. The herds grow thin, and the men ride out further every day to find pasture. Some families settle near villages to trade for grain. The elders say the sky is angry; the young ones listen to the shaman's drum at night, waiting for rain.",
  "huns:quake": "In January 447, as the Hun army was advancing in the Balkans, a great earthquake struck Constantinople (Κωνσταντινούπολις) and brought down long stretches of its walls and fifty-seven towers. News raced through the Hun camps that the city's defences had fallen. But the citizens rebuilt the walls in barely sixty days, working day and night. The Hun riders who rode up to see the gap found only new stone, and turned back.",
  "huns:winter": "Imagined: in winter the Danube freezes, and Hun riders can cross on the ice. On the plain, families shelter in felt tents and wooden huts, wrapped in furs and the skins of field mice, as one Roman writer claimed. Horses paw through the snow for dry grass. At night the old ones tell stories of the eastern steppe and its endless winters, and the children huddle close.",
  "huns:plague": "In 452 the Huns invaded Italy, but their army fell sick. The chronicler Hydatius writes that the Huns suffered from disease and famine sent from heaven, and were struck by the soldiers of the eastern emperor. Hun warriors who had crossed the Alps in triumph now buried companions every day. They went home across the mountains, fewer and quieter, with little to celebrate.",
  "huns:wedding": "On his journey in 449, Priscus (Πρίσκος) came to a village where Attila had stopped to marry the daughter of a man named Eskam (Ἐσκάμ), though he already had many wives. Priscus does not describe the ceremony, but he saw how the Huns feasted: women singing in lines under linen veils, food brought on silver trays, wine and kamon (κάμος), a barley drink, poured freely. Imagined: the bride rides to her husband's camp, the village sings behind her.",

  // ---------------- Cumans: more scenes ----------------
  "cumans:siege": "Imagined: Mongol riders have cut a Cuman clan off on the open steppe in 1237. There is nowhere to run, so the families draw their wagons into a ring, chained wheel to wheel, with the women, children and herds inside. The men shoot from between the wagons. Water runs low in the leather bags; the horses grow weak. On the third night the old men sing to the ancestors, and the young men prepare for the last ride out.",
  "cumans:triumph": "Imagined: Cuman riders come home across the steppe after a long raid, driving horses and carrying cloth. The camp comes out to meet them: children run alongside, women sing, and a fresh skin of kumys is opened. That evening there is a feast, and a singer with the kobyz tells the deeds of the day. The riders give gifts to the old and to widows. The best warrior is honoured with the first cup.",
  "cumans:build": "Across the Pontic steppe the Cumans raised stone statues on the burial mounds of their ancestors: men and women carved from limestone, holding a cup in both hands, with braids, belts and earrings carefully shown. Families worked together to cut and haul the stone and raise it on the kurgan. Travellers wrote that Cumans left offerings before them. Many still stand, looking east over the grass.",
  "cumans:voyage": "In 1239, fleeing the Mongols, Köten led some forty thousand Cuman families west into Hungary, crossing the Carpathians with their wagons, horses and cattle. The chronicler Roger of Torre Maggiore (Rogerius) describes the long column winding down from the mountain passes. They were given land on the great plain. For generations Cumans kept their language, their prayers and their songs, and their descendants still remember their steppe origins.",
  "cumans:festival": "Imagined: in spring, when the mares give their first milk, a Cuman camp holds a feast of the new kumys. Everyone gathers before the chief's yurt; the elders pour the first cup onto the ground for the spirits and the ancestors, then pass it round. There are songs, kobyz and dombra, and the young ones dance. After the long winter, the white milk means life is returning to the steppe.",
  "cumans:games": "Imagined: at a Cuman gathering, riders play kokpar: a goat carcass is thrown into a field, and dozens of horsemen fight to grab it, drag it across the grass, and throw it into a goal. Horses collide, men lean from the saddle, the crowd roars. Others race over long distances, or wrestle on horseback, trying to pull each other down. The winners are honoured with horses and songs.",
  "cumans:drought": "Imagined: a dry summer on the Pontic steppe. The wells are low, the grass crackles underfoot, and dust rises behind every rider. Cuman families move their herds toward the rivers, but other clans have come too. The elders meet to share the water. At night a shaman beats his drum and calls on Tengri, the blue sky, to send rain. Everyone waits, watching the horizon for clouds.",
  "cumans:quake": "Imagined: Cuman traders have come down to the Crimean coast to sell horses and furs. One morning the earth shakes; stone houses crack, walls fall into the street, the sea pulls back from the beach. The Cumans run out onto open ground, holding their horses. Later, back on the steppe, they tell their families how the solid earth moved like water, and the old women make signs against evil.",
  "cumans:winter": "Imagined: a jut, a killing winter, comes to the Cumans. Rain freezes into a crust of ice over the snow, and the horses cannot break through to the grass. Day after day the animals weaken and die. Families gather in their yurts, burning dung, wrapping the lambs in their own coats. The men ride out to find pasture where the wind has cleared the ice. In spring they count what is left, and start again.",
  "cumans:plague": "Imagined: sickness has broken out in a Cuman camp. The shaman beats his drum all night around the tents, chasing the evil spirit. The families of the sick move their yurts apart from the others, and no one shares a cup. Riders carry warnings to the next camps. When the sickness passes, the people burn the bedding of the dead and raise a new stone figure on the burial mound.",
  "cumans:wedding": "Imagined: a Cuman bride is taken from her family's camp to the groom's. Before she leaves, she sings a farewell song to her parents and sisters, and everyone weeps. At the groom's yurt, her face is covered with a veil; a singer improvises verses, introducing her to each relative, and only at the end lifts the veil. Then there is kumys, meat and music, and the young people play games until dawn.",


  // ---------------- Tatars: more scenes ----------------
  "tatars:siege": "In the autumn of 1236 the Mongol army surrounded Bilyar Биләр, the greatest city of the Volga Bulgars, ancestors of the Kazan Tatars. Its people, merchants, potters and farmers, crowded behind wooden walls and ditches, and held out for forty-five days. When the city fell, it was burned. Survivors fled north into the forests and rebuilt their lives. Tatar legends still tell of the lost city and its bells beneath the earth.",
  "tatars:triumph": "Imagined: the riders of a Tatar village come home from a long, hard campaign. At the edge of the village the women wait with bread and salt and embroidered towels, and the children run to hold the horses. That evening there is a feast: honey, mutton, chak-chak чәкчәк sweets. An old man plays the kurai курай, and the men sing a bäyet бәет, a ballad, so the day will be remembered.",
  "tatars:build": "When a Tatar family needed a house, a barn or a mosque, the village held an ömä өмә, a day of shared work. Neighbours came with axes and horses; men hewed the logs and raised the walls, women cooked in big kettles and sang. In autumn there was a goose-plucking ömä, with girls carrying the geese to the river in a procession. Afterwards everyone ate together, and the young danced to the accordion.",
  "tatars:voyage": "Imagined: a Tatar merchant leaves Kazan Казан in spring with a caravan bound for Bukhara Бохара, carrying leather, furs and iron goods. For weeks the camels plod across the steppe and the desert, past wells and caravanserais. He prays five times a day and writes letters home in Arabic script. When the blue domes of Bukhara rise on the horizon, the whole caravan cheers. In autumn he will return with silk and tea.",
  "tatars:festival": "Nardugan Нардуган, the Tatar midwinter feast, comes when the sun turns back toward spring. Young people dress up in masks and sheepskins turned inside out and go from house to house singing and joking; they are given sweets and treats. Girls tell fortunes with wax dropped into water, or listen at windows for a word that will name their future husband. Then there are sleigh rides and dancing to the accordion until late.",
  "tatars:games": "Tatar children play Ak tiräk, kük tiräk Ак тирәк, күк тирәк, white poplar, blue poplar. Two lines hold hands and face each other; one side calls out a name, and that child runs full speed at the other line, trying to break through the joined hands. If he breaks through, he takes a player back; if not, he stays. The game goes on with shrieks and laughter until one line is gone and the sun is down.",
  "tatars:drought": "In 1891 the rains failed along the Volga and the harvest died in the fields. In Tatar villages, families ate bread made with chaff, acorns and goosefoot; cattle were sold for almost nothing, and many children died over the winter. Mosques collected grain for the poor, and merchants in Kazan Казан raised money for soup kitchens. The old people called it the year of hunger, ачлык елы, and spoke of it quietly for the rest of their lives.",
  "tatars:quake": "Imagined: Tatar families in a Crimean village are woken one night by the roar of the earth. Stone walls crack, a minaret falls, and in the morning they find the springs flowing muddy. The imam calls people into the open. Families camp in the orchards for a week, cooking together and praying, until the ground stops trembling. Then they rebuild, stone by stone.",
  "tatars:winter": "Imagined: a buran буран, a snowstorm, sweeps over a Tatar village on the Volga. Snow piles to the windows, and the road to Kazan Казан is lost for days. Inside the wooden house, the family sits around the big stove, drinking tea with milk and eating chak-chak. Grandmother tells the tale of Shüräle Шүрәле, the forest spirit, and the children shiver happily. In the morning they dig a path to the barn.",
  "tatars:plague": "Imagined: in 1346 a terrible sickness spreads along the Volga (Идел), and the trading towns fall silent. In one Tatar settlement, families shut their doors, and the mullah goes from house to house reciting prayers for the dying. The dead are washed and buried quickly, as faith requires, but there are too many. Survivors leave food on the doorsteps of the sick. The bazaar is empty, and the caravans no longer come.",
  "tatars:wedding": "At a Tatar wedding, the nikah никах is read by the mullah at the bride's home. Days later the groom fetches her with decorated sleighs or carts, and her relatives block the road until gifts are given. In the morning after she arrives, the young wife walks to the spring with her yoke and buckets, the su yuly су юлы, the water path, and drops a coin in the water, to belong to her new village.",

  // ---------------- Kipchak: more scenes ----------------
  "golden-horde:siege": "In 1395 Timur's army swept into the lands of the Kipchak and reached Sarai سراي on the Volga. Families who could not flee hid in the city's houses and gardens, behind walls that had never been built for war. The city was looted and burned; the craftsmen were driven away. Survivors returned to a city of ash and broken tiles. The great capital never recovered, and the steppe slowly took it back.",
  "golden-horde:triumph": "Imagined: the khan's army returns to the steppe after a long campaign. Riders gallop ahead to the camp, shouting the news. The great white tent is opened, the naqqara drums are beaten, and karnay horns sound. Kumys flows from leather skins; sheep and horses are roasted. Singers and storytellers tell of the battle, and the warriors receive gifts of horses, silk and silver from the khan's hand.",
  "golden-horde:build": "When Ibn Battuta ابن بطوطة came to New Sarai سراي on the Volga in the 1330s, he called it one of the most beautiful cities, so large that it took half a day to walk across. Kipchak, Mongol, Circassian, Russian and Byzantine traders each had their own quarter, with markets, mosques, and gardens watered by canals. Builders made it of fired brick and glazed tiles, a city of the steppe that stood on one spot.",
  "golden-horde:voyage": "In 1334 Ibn Battuta ابن بطوطة travelled with Bayalun, a wife of Özbeg Khan and daughter of the Byzantine emperor, who was going home to Constantinople to give birth. Her caravan of wagons crossed the Kipchak steppe with five hundred riders, slave girls and servants, and Kipchak cooks who made her meals in moving kitchens. At the frontier the Kipchak escort turned back. Ibn Battuta wrote that the princess wept when the steppe was left behind.",
  "golden-horde:festival": "Ibn Battuta ابن بطوطة spent Eid al-Fitr عيد الفطر at Özbeg Khan's camp. He wrote that the khan rode out with his army in fine armour, the khatuns in their wagons covered with silk, and that a great feast followed in a pavilion of gold cloth. Kumys and meat were served to everyone, and the khan sat on a throne with his wives beside him. The steppe rang with music all day.",
  "golden-horde:games": "Imagined: at a Kipchak gathering, the best riders of every clan race over many kilometres of open steppe, with boys as jockeys, small and light on bareback horses. Wrestlers grapple in front of the khan's tent, archers shoot at targets, and riders snatch coins from the ground at full gallop. The crowd shouts the names of their champions. The winners receive horses, robes and the khan's own cup of kumys.",
  "golden-horde:drought": "Imagined: the summer rains do not come to the Kipchak steppe. The grass turns brown, the lakes shrink to salt crusts, and the herds weaken. Families move their wagons north toward the forests, or toward the great rivers, looking for grazing. At night they pray to Tengri and to God, and pour a little milk on the ground. When the first storm finally comes, everyone runs out into the rain, laughing.",
  "golden-horde:quake": "Imagined: Kipchak merchants in the Crimean town of Solkhat feel the ground shake one evening. The minaret sways, plaster falls, and people run into the streets. Camels bellow in the caravanserai. By morning the damage is counted: a few houses down, a well cracked, the mosque's dome split. The townspeople help each other, and the khan's governor sends masons to rebuild.",
  "golden-horde:winter": "Ibn Battuta ابن بطوطة crossed the Kipchak steppe in winter. He wrote that he wore three fur coats and two pairs of trousers, and boots lined with fur, and that the water froze on his face when he washed. He could not climb onto his horse without help. Kipchak travellers laughed and helped him, and he crossed the frozen Volga on the ice to Sarai سراي, its frozen river busy with sledges.",
  "golden-horde:plague": "In 1346 the Kipchak army surrounding the trading port of Caffa (Kefe) was struck by plague. Soldiers sickened and died in their thousands; the camp filled with the dead and dying. Gabriele de' Mussis wrote later of how the bodies were thrown over the walls. For the families on the steppe, the warriors who did not return were never forgotten; the sickness spread along the trade routes into every town of the steppe.",
  "golden-horde:wedding": "Imagined: a Kipchak bride leaves her parents' camp, singing a farewell song as her mother weeps. Her dowry rides with her in a decorated wagon. At the groom's camp she is hidden behind a curtain until a singer, verse by verse, introduces her to each of the groom's relatives, and they give gifts. Then the curtain is lifted, kumys is poured, and the young people play and sing late into the night.",


  // ---------------- Amazigh: more scenes ----------------
  "berbers:siege": "Imagined: soldiers are camped below an Amazigh village on a ridge in Kabylia, Tamurt n Leqbayel ⵜⴰⵎⵓⵔⵜ ⵏ ⵍⵇⴱⴰⵢⴻⵍ. The paths are watched, no grain comes in, and the springs below are out of reach. Women carry water up at night by hidden tracks; the men hold the narrow approach with old muskets. The villagers eat dried figs and acorn bread. In the evenings the women sing izlan ⵉⵣⵍⴰⵏ, short songs of courage, and the men answer from the walls.",
  "berbers:triumph": "Imagined: Tuareg men ride home across the desert on their white camels, faces veiled in indigo. At the camp the women run out ululating; that night is an ahal, an evening of music and courtship. A woman draws her bow across the imzad ⵉⵎⵣⴰⴷ while the others sing and beat the tende ⵜⴻⵏⴷⴻ drum. The riders make their camels dance around the circle. A man is only praised if the women sing of him.",
  "berbers:build": "In the Anti-Atlas, Amazigh villages built igudar ⵉⴳⵓⴷⴰⵔ, fortified collective granaries, on rocky heights. Everyone worked together to raise the stone walls; each family received a small room for its grain, olive oil, honey and documents, opened with a carved wooden key. A guardian, the lamin, kept the gate, and written rules hung inside. In times of danger, the granary was the safest place in the valley.",
  "berbers:voyage": "Each autumn the Tuareg ⵉⵎⵓⵀⴰⵖ of the Aïr mountains set out on the azalai, the salt caravan, to the oasis of Bilma, weeks across the Ténéré desert. Hundreds of camels walk in long lines; the men navigate by stars, dunes and the colour of the sand. They carry millet and cloth and return with salt and dates. When the green palms of Bilma finally rise over the horizon, the men sing.",
  "berbers:festival": "On 12 or 13 January, Amazigh families celebrate Yennayer ⵢⴻⵏⵏⴰⵢⴻⵔ, the new year of the farmers' calendar. The women cook couscous with seven vegetables, or a dish with a single date stone hidden in it: whoever finds it will be lucky all year. Children go from house to house collecting sweets and dried fruit. Houses are cleaned, the hearth is renewed, and families pray for a good harvest.",
  "berbers:games": "Imagined: at a Tuareg festival in the desert, young men race their camels across the sand while women sit in a circle beating the tende ⵜⴻⵏⴷⴻ and singing. The riders gallop, then swing back to circle the women, showing off their camels and their indigo robes. The best rider earns more than a prize: the women's praise in song, which will be repeated around the fires for months.",
  "berbers:drought": "In the 1970s, years of drought struck the Sahara and the Sahel. Tuareg ⵉⵎⵓⵀⴰⵖ herders watched their camels and goats die along the dry wells; many families left the desert for the towns, or for exile far from home. Young men far from their camps learned the guitar and sang of assouf ⴰⵙⵓⴼ, longing and loneliness, of the desert and the families they had lost. Those songs became a new music.",
  "berbers:quake": "Just before midnight on 29 February 1960, during Ramadan, an earthquake struck the city of Agadir ⴰⴳⴰⴷⵉⵔ. In fifteen seconds most of the city fell, and the old kasbah on the hill collapsed. Around fifteen thousand people died, many asleep in their homes. Families from the Amazigh villages of the Souss ⵙⵓⵙ came down to search for relatives and dig through the rubble. The city was rebuilt further south.",
  "berbers:winter": "Imagined: in the High Atlas ⴰⴷⵔⴰⵔ ⵏ ⴷⵔⵏ, snow closes the passes and the village is cut off for weeks. Inside the earthen house, the family sleeps near the animals for warmth. Women weave carpets at the loom by the fire and sing ahidus songs, men mend tools, and everyone drinks sweet mint tea. Barley and dried meat from autumn must last until spring. When the mule track opens again, the whole village celebrates.",
  "berbers:plague": "Imagined: news of plague comes to an Amazigh ksar in the Draa valley. The elders close the gate to strangers; no caravans are allowed in. Women burn herbs in the houses, and the fqih (فقيه) reads the Qur'an (القرآن) at the mosque day and night. When someone falls ill, one family member cares for him while the rest keep apart. The whole village waits, listening for news on the wind.",
  "berbers:wedding": "In September, the Ait Hdiddou ⴰⵢⵜ ⵃⴷⵉⴷⵓ of Imilchil ⵉⵎⵉⵍⵛⵉⵍ hold their famous marriage gathering. Legend says two lovers from rival clans, Isli ⵉⵙⵍⵉ and Tislit ⵜⵉⵙⵍⵉⵜ, wept so much when forbidden to marry that their tears made two lakes. Now young men and women meet freely at the moussem, choose each other, and register their marriages, while the bendir drums beat and the ahidus dancers sway.",

  // ---------------- Habesha: more scenes ----------------
  "ethiopians:siege": "Imagined: raiders are in the valley, and the Habesha villagers drive their cattle and carry their grain up onto the amba አምባ, a flat-topped mountain with sheer cliffs. They pull up the ropes behind them. Water is hauled up in jars; the priest has brought the tabot ታቦት, the church's holy tablet, wrapped in cloth. For weeks they wait on the summit, praying, singing psalms, and watching the fires below.",
  "ethiopians:triumph": "Imagined: Habesha warriors return to their village after a battle. The women come out to meet them with the high ululation, elelta እልልታ. One by one the men step forward and perform fukera ፉከራ, a boasting chant: they stamp, wave their spears, and shout their deeds and their family names. The crowd answers with shouts. That night tej ጠጅ, honey wine, is poured, and an azmari አዝማሪ improvises songs about each of them.",
  "ethiopians:build": "At Aksum አክሱም, between the 3rd and 4th centuries, Habesha stonecutters carved great granite stelae, tall as towers, with false doors and windows like multi-storey houses. Hundreds of workers dragged them from the quarry on rollers and raised them with ramps and ropes. The greatest, over thirty metres, fell and broke, perhaps as it was being raised. Its fragments still lie where it fell, beside the stelae that stand.",
  "ethiopians:voyage": "The Kebra Nagast ክብረ ነገሥት tells how Makeda ማክዳ, Queen of Sheba, travelled from her land to Jerusalem with a caravan of camels loaded with gold, spices and precious stones, to meet King Solomon and learn his wisdom. She stayed six months, and returned home carrying a son, Menelik ምኒልክ. For Habesha people, her journey is the beginning of a long story, told by priests and sung by azmaris.",
  "ethiopians:festival": "Enkutatash እንቁጣጣሽ, the Habesha new year, falls in September, when the long rains end and yellow meskel flowers cover the hills. Girls in new white dresses go from house to house singing Abebayehosh አበባየሆሽ and offering bouquets or drawings; boys carry pictures they have painted. Families give them bread or coins, and everyone eats together. It is a feast of fresh beginnings and green fields.",
  "ethiopians:games": "On Genna ገና, Christmas, young Habesha men play the game also called genna: a hockey-like game with curved sticks and a wooden ball, played across open fields until dusk. Legend says the shepherds were playing it when they heard of the birth of Christ. The village elders watch and shout, the losers sometimes sing teasing songs, and everyone shares a feast afterwards.",
  "ethiopians:drought": "Imagined: in a Habesha village in the highlands of Wollo ወሎ, the small belg rains have failed and now the big kiremt rains are late too. The teff ጤፍ seedlings yellow in the fields. Every morning the women look to the sky, every evening the men count the grain left in the store. The priests carry the tabot around the church; the whole village fasts and prays. When the clouds finally gather, people weep.",
  "ethiopians:quake": "Imagined: in Ankober አንኮበር, on the edge of the Rift Valley, the ground shakes one night. Stone walls crack, the church bell rings by itself, and people run out of their houses into the cold. The priests lead everyone in prayer. In the morning, families find cattle missing and a few homes ruined. Neighbours bring injera እንጀራ and help rebuild. The old people say the earth is restless here.",
  "ethiopians:plague": "In 1887 cattle plague arrived on the Red Sea coast and spread into the Habesha highlands. Within two years most of the cattle were dead. For farmers who ploughed with oxen, it was a catastrophe: fields went unploughed, milk vanished, and famine followed. Families wept over their animals as over relatives. In some villages men pulled the ploughs themselves. The time is still remembered as the beginning of Kifu Qen (ክፉ ቀን).",
  "ethiopians:wedding": "At a Habesha wedding, the groom and his mize ሚዜ, his best men, come to the bride's house singing. Her family closes the gate, and the two sides trade teasing songs until the groom is let in. Elelta rises from the women, the kebero ከበሮ drum beats, and the shoulders start to dance in eskista እስክስታ. At the feast, everyone eats injera with doro wat and drinks tej, honey wine, late into the night.",


  // ---------------- Mande: more scenes ----------------
  "malians:siege": "Imagined: a Mande town in the savanna is ringed by its tata, a thick wall of sun-dried earth. Horsemen have surrounded it, and the farmers' families have crowded inside with their grain and goats. At night the defenders keep watch from the wall while the drums sound, so the enemy knows they are awake. The jeli ߖߋߟߌ, the griot, sings the deeds of their ancestors at the gate. Food grows short; still they hold.",
  "malians:triumph": "After the battle of Kirina, around 1235, the epic of Sunjata ߛߎ߲ߖߊߕߊ tells that the Mande clans gathered at Kurukan Fuga ߞߎߙߎߞߊ߲ߝߎߜߊ. Sunjata's griot Balla Fasseke stood before the crowd and sang his praises; each clan's chief planted his spear in the ground before the king. There was music for days, the balafon ringing, as the families came home to the land of their fathers.",
  "malians:build": "Each spring, before the rains, the people of Djenné ߘߖߋߣߋ replaster their Great Mosque, the largest building of mud in the world. For days beforehand, men mix mud with rice husks in pits; on the day, hundreds of young men climb the walls on palm-wood beams while others pass up baskets, racing to finish first. Women carry water, elders direct the work, and drums and songs keep everyone moving.",
  "malians:voyage": "Al-Umari (العمري) wrote that Mansa Musa (منسا موسى) told him in Cairo (القاهرة) how his predecessor, Abubakari, wanted to know where the western ocean ended. He sent two hundred ships; only one came back, saying the others had vanished in a great current. Then the king himself set out with two thousand ships, leaving Musa to rule. He was never seen again. Mande griots still wonder what land he found.",
  "malians:festival": "Every year in the town of San ߛߊ߲, on a chosen day before the rains, the people hold the Sanké mon ߛߊ߲ߞߋ ߡߐ߲, the collective fishing of the sacred pond. At a signal, hundreds of men, women and children rush into the water with nets and gourds and fish together for hours. The drums beat and the women sing. Afterwards a share of the catch goes to every family, and the masks dance.",
  "malians:games": "Imagined: after the harvest, the young men of a Mande village wrestle in a ring of sand. The djembe ߖߋ߲ߓߋ and dundun drums call each pair in, and the wrestlers dance around one another before they grip. Girls clap and sing teasing verses for the boys they favour. When a champion throws his opponent, the jeli ߖߋߟߌ shouts his family praise names, and the crowd surges forward.",
  "malians:drought": "In the early 1970s the rains failed across the Sahel for years in a row. Mande farmers watched their millet wither in the fields; the Niger (Jɔliba) river shrank, fish became scarce, and herders' cattle died along the dry tracks. Many families left their villages for the towns. Women walked hours each day to find water. Griots made songs of those years, so the young would know what their parents survived.",
  "malians:plague": "Imagined: in a Mande village, a fever spreads through the compounds in the dry season. The elders send the children to relatives in another village. The healer gathers leaves and roots; the imam reads prayers at night. When someone dies, the women wail and the men dig the grave together. The jeli sings softly at the funerals. When the fever ends, the village holds a sacrifice and a feast of thanks.",
  "malians:wedding": "At a Mande wedding, the bride is prepared for days by the women of her family, her hands and feet dyed with henna, her hair braided. On the day, the jeli ߖߋߟߌ sings the praises of both families, naming ancestors back many generations, while the kora ߞߐߙߊ and balafon play. Guests give gifts, and the griot announces each one aloud. Then the bride is led to her husband's compound, singing and dancing.",

  // ---------------- Hausa: more scenes ----------------
  "hausa:siege": "Imagined: an enemy army is camped outside Kano كَنُو. The gates in the great earthen wall, the ganuwa, are shut, and villagers from the countryside have crowded inside with their grain and goats. The wall encloses farmland and wells, so the city can feed itself for months. Each night the defenders watch from the wall, the kalangu drums speaking along it. Inside, the women grind grain and the children sleep close.",
  "hausa:triumph": "Imagined: the men of a Hausa town ride home after a hard campaign. The women come out with high ululation, and the maroka, the praise singers, run beside the horses shouting each rider's family names and deeds. The kalangu drums answer from the gate. In the compounds, the women cook rice and stew, and gifts of cloth and kola nuts are shared. A man praised by the maroka today will be remembered in songs for years.",
  "hausa:build": "The Kano Chronicle says that around the 12th century, Sarki Gijimasu began the city wall of Kano كَنُو, and later rulers made it greater. Thousands worked together: they dug a deep ditch, piled the earth into a wall many metres high, and plastered it with mud. Builders sang as they worked, and the wall eventually surrounded the city and its farmland. Parts of it still stand today.",
  "hausa:voyage": "For centuries, Hausa pilgrims walked to Mecca (مكة) on the hajj, across the Sahel and the Sudan to the Red Sea. The journey could take years: they stopped to farm and trade to pay their way, and some never went further, founding Hausa villages along the route. When at last they saw the Red Sea, and crossed to Arabia, they wept with joy. Their descendants still live along that road.",
  "hausa:festival": "Each year at Argungu, on the banks of the Sokoto (Sakkwato) river, thousands of fishermen wait on the bank for the signal. When it sounds, they leap into the water with nets and calabash floats, fishing by hand for the great Nile perch. Drummers beat on the shore, praise singers shout the names of the winners, and boats with musicians move along the river. The catch is weighed, and the whole town feasts.",
  "hausa:games": "Dambe, Hausa boxing, was once a contest of butchers' guilds at harvest time. One fighter's strong hand is wrapped in cord; the other hand is open to defend. Fighters come out with a lucky charm, the drummers beat the rhythm of their village, and the crowd chants. Rounds end when someone falls or the elders call a halt. Champions earned fame and gifts, and praise singers made songs about them.",
  "hausa:drought": "Imagined: the rains are late, and the millet in the fields of Hausa farmers has stopped growing. The wells are low, and women queue before dawn. The imam leads the community in prayer for rain outside the town; everyone wears old clothes. Families eat less and save seed for the next year. At night, the old men talk of earlier famines and how their grandparents survived.",
  "hausa:plague": "Imagined: at the height of the dry season, when the harmattan blows, meningitis strikes a Hausa town. Children and young people fall ill with fever and stiff necks, and the healers cannot keep up. The mallam (malam) writes Qur'an (القرآن) verses on wooden boards, washes off the ink, and gives the water to drink. Families keep watch through the night. When the rains come, the sickness fades, and the town mourns its dead.",
  "hausa:wedding": "A Hausa wedding begins with the families agreeing the sadaki, the bride price. Days before, the bride's friends paint her hands and feet with henna, kunshi, and sing to her. The marriage is sealed at the mosque, where the men read the Fatiha فَاتِحَة. In the evening the bride is taken to her husband's house, her friends singing, drummers and praise singers following. Then comes a feast with dancing.",
});
